   288	## 4. LEVEL 3: FULL DOCUMENTATION (LOC GUIDANCE: ≥500)
   289	
   290	### When to Use
   291	
   292	- Complex features, architecture changes, major decisions
   293	- High complexity
   294	- Multiple systems or components involved
   295	- Requires coordination across teams
   296	- Significant architectural impact
   297	- Major technical decisions need to be documented
   298	
   299	### Required Files (Level 2 + decision-record)
   300	
   301	- `spec.md` (from Level 2) - Requirements and user stories
   302	- `plan.md` (from Level 2) - Technical implementation plan
   303	- `tasks.md` (from Level 2) - Task breakdown by user story
   304	- `acceptance-criteria.md` (from Level 2) - the criteria that decide whether the packet may close
   305	- `decision-record.md` (NEW at Level 3) - Architecture Decision Records/ADRs
   306	- `implementation-summary.md` (from Level 2) - Post-implementation outcomes and verification evidence
   307	
   308	### Optional Files
   309	
   310	- `resource-map.md` - lean path catalog (cross-cutting, see §9)
   311	- `research/research.md` - Comprehensive research documentation
   312	
   313	### Content Expectations
   314	
   315	**All Level 2 content expectations PLUS:**
   316	
   317	**decision-record.md required sections:**
   318	- Context and problem
   319	- Options considered (2-4 typically)
   320	- Decision made
   321	- Rationale
   322	- Consequences and trade-offs
   323	
   324	**Enforcement:** Hard block if `decision-record.md` missing
   325	
   326	### Example Scenarios
   327	
   328	**Good fits for Level 3:**
   329	- Major feature implementation (user dashboard with analytics)
   330	- System redesign (payment flow v2)
   331	- Architecture changes (microservices migration)
   332	- Multi-team projects (integration with external systems)
   333	- New product vertical (marketplace feature)
   334	- Performance overhaul (real-time collaboration)
   335	- Database or framework choices
   336	- Major refactoring approaches
   337	
   338	### Template Sources
   339	
   340	**Manifest templates (scaffolded by `create.sh`):**
   341	- `templates/core/spec.md.tmpl`
   342	- `templates/core/plan.md.tmpl`
   343	- `templates/core/tasks.md.tmpl`
   344	- `templates/addons/acceptance-criteria.md.tmpl`
   345	- `templates/addons/decision-record.md.tmpl`
   346	- `templates/core/implementation-summary.md.tmpl`
   347	
   348	**Addendum source (+Arch):**
   349	- `templates/core/spec.md.tmpl`
   350	- `templates/core/plan.md.tmpl`
   351	- `templates/core/tasks.md.tmpl`
   352	
   353	**Optional:**
   354	- `templates/addons/research.md.tmpl` (render into `research/research.md` when needed)
   355	
   356	### Template Adaptation
   357	
   358	**All Level 2 adaptations PLUS:**
   359	
   360	**decision-record.md:**
   361	1. Document context and problem clearly
   362	2. Present 2-4 viable options (not every possible choice)
   363	3. Fair comparison (pros/cons for each)
   364	4. State clear decision with rationale
   365	5. Document trade-offs honestly
   366	6. Note what was sacrificed for chosen path
   367	7. Use the canonical required filename `decision-record.md` (topic-specific ADR files are supplemental)
   368	8. Remove all sample content
   369	
   370	---
   371	
   372	## 5. LEVEL 3+: EXTENDED DOCUMENTATION (COMPLEXITY SCORE: 80-100)
   373	
   374	### When to Use
   375	
   376	Level 3+ is auto-detected via complexity scoring for highly complex tasks:
   377	- Multi-agent coordination required
   378	- Complex architectural changes with high stakes
   379	- Large-scale migrations or rewrites
   380	- Tasks requiring explicit AI execution protocols
   381	- High coordination needs across multiple systems
   382	
   383	### Required Files (Level 3 + Extended Features)
   384	
   385	- `spec.md` (from Level 3) - With Complexity Assessment section
   386	- `plan.md` (from Level 3) - With AI Execution Framework, dependency graphs
   387	- `tasks.md` (from Level 3) - With 3-Tier Task Format, AI Execution Protocol
   388	- `acceptance-criteria.md` (from Level 3) - extended criteria with a sign-off section
   389	- `decision-record.md` (from Level 3) - Architecture Decision Records
   390	- `implementation-summary.md` (from Level 3) - Required completion artifact for all levels
   391	
   392	### Optional Files
   393	
   394	- `resource-map.md` - lean path catalog (cross-cutting, see §9)
   395	
   396	### Extended Features (Auto-Enabled)
   397	
   398	| Feature | Description | Template Section |
   399	|---------|-------------|------------------|
   400	| AI Execution Protocol | Pre-task checklist, execution rules, status format | tasks.md, plan.md |
   401	| Dependency Graph | Full DAG visualization of task dependencies | plan.md |
   402	| Effort Estimation | Story points/hours per phase with totals | plan.md |
   403	| Extended verification | 100-150 items with P0/P1/P2 prioritization | tasks.md |
   404	| Sign-Off Section | Technical Lead, Product Owner, QA Lead sign-offs | tasks.md |
   405	| Workstream Organization | Parallel workstream tracking and coordination | tasks.md |
   406	
   407	### AI Execution Protocol Components
   408	
   409	**Pre-Task Checklist (9 steps):**
   410	1. Load spec.md and verify scope hasn't changed
   411	2. Load plan.md and identify current phase
   412	3. Load tasks.md and find next uncompleted task
   413	4. Verify task dependencies are satisfied
   414	5. Load tasks.md and identify relevant P0/P1 verification items
   415	6. Check for blocking issues in decision-record.md
   416	7. Verify handover.md and implementation-summary.md for context from previous sessions
   417	8. Confirm understanding of success criteria
   418	9. Begin implementation only after all checks pass
   419	
   420	**Execution Rules Table:**
   421	| Rule | Description |
   422	|------|-------------|
   423	| TASK-SEQ | Complete tasks in dependency order |
   424	| TASK-SCOPE | Stay within task boundary, no scope creep |
   425	| TASK-VERIFY | Verify each task against acceptance criteria |
   426	| TASK-DOC | Update status immediately on completion |
   427	
   428	**Status Reporting Format:**
   429	```
   430	## Status Update - [TIMESTAMP]
   431	- **Task**: T### - [Description]
   432	- **Status**: [IN_PROGRESS | COMPLETED | BLOCKED]
   433	- **Evidence**: [Link to code/test/artifact]
   434	- **Blockers**: [None | Description]
   435	- **Next**: T### - [Next task]
   436	```
   437	
   438	### Content Expectations
   439	
   440	**All Level 3 content expectations PLUS:**
   441	
   442	**spec.md additions:**
   443	- Complexity Assessment table with 5-dimension scores
   444	- Executive Summary (2-3 paragraphs for stakeholders)
   445	
   446	**plan.md additions:**
   447	- AI Execution Framework section
   448	- Full dependency graph (ASCII or DAG)
   449	- Effort estimation per phase
   450	- Phase execution flowcharts (complex phases)
   451	
   452	**tasks.md additions:**
   453	- 3-Tier Task Format with full metadata
   454	- AI Execution Protocol section
   455	- Workstream organization (if parallel work)
   456	- Status reporting format
   457	
   458	**tasks.md verification additions:**
   459	- Extended Verification section (Level 3+)
   460	- Sign-Off section with Technical Lead, Product Owner, QA Lead
   461	- Verification Summary table
   462	
   463	### Example Scenarios
   464	
   465	**Good fits for Level 3+:**
   466	- Multi-agent implementation with 10+ parallel workstreams
   467	- Platform migration affecting 50+ files
   468	- Security audit remediation with compliance requirements
   469	- Real-time collaboration feature with complex state management
   470	- Database migration with zero-downtime requirements
   471	- API versioning with backward compatibility constraints
   472	
   473	### Template Sources
   474	
   475	**Manifest templates (scaffolded by `create.sh`):**
   476	- `templates/core/spec.md.tmpl`
   477	- `templates/core/plan.md.tmpl`
   478	- `templates/core/tasks.md.tmpl`
   479	- `templates/addons/acceptance-criteria.md.tmpl`
   480	- `templates/addons/decision-record.md.tmpl`
   481	- `templates/core/implementation-summary.md.tmpl`
   482	
   483	**Addendum source (+Govern):**
   484	- `templates/core/spec.md.tmpl`
   485	- `templates/core/plan.md.tmpl`
   486	- `templates/core/tasks.md.tmpl`
   487	
   488	**Optional:**
   489	- `templates/addons/research.md.tmpl` (render into `research/research.md` when needed)
   490	
   491	### Creating Level 3+ Spec Folder
   492	
   493	```bash
   494	# Specify level directly (recommended)
   495	bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/create.sh "Complex migration" --level 3+
   496	```
   497	
   498	### Template Adaptation
   499	
   500	**All Level 3 adaptations PLUS:**
   501	
   502	1. Fill Complexity Assessment table with actual scores
   503	2. Write Executive Summary for stakeholder communication
   504	3. Create AI Execution Protocol with project-specific rules
   505	4. Build dependency graph showing all task relationships
   506	5. Add effort estimates per phase
   507	6. Expand checklist to 100+ items covering all validation areas
   508	7. Add approval tracking section with reviewers
   509	8. Organize workstreams if parallel work streams exist
   510	9. Remove all sample content and placeholders
   511	
   512	### Coordination-Root Profile
   513	
   514	When the spec folder serves as a coordination root for a multi-phase effort, Level 3+ additionally requires:
   515	- Point-in-time directory snapshot (total dirs, top-level, numbered)
   516	- Direct-phase documentation map with current status values
   517	- ADR record establishing source-of-truth precedence (e.g., current tree truth over historical synthesis)
   518	
   519	---
   520	
   521	## 6. LEVEL MIGRATION
   522	
   523	### Script-Assisted Upgrade (Recommended)
   524	
   525	Use `upgrade-level.sh` to upgrade existing spec folders to a higher documentation level:
   526	
   527	```bash
   528	# Upgrade to Level 2 (auto-detects current level)
   529	bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/upgrade-level.sh specs/042-feature/ --to 2
   530	
   531	# Upgrade to Level 3 (chains through intermediate levels automatically)
   532	bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/upgrade-level.sh specs/042-feature/ --to 3
   533	
   534	# Upgrade to Level 3+ (approval workflow + compliance)
   535	bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/upgrade-level.sh specs/042-feature/ --to 3+
   536	
   537	# Preview changes without modifying files
   538	bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/upgrade-level.sh specs/042-feature/ --to 3 --dry-run
   539	```
   540	
   541	**Supported upgrade paths:**
   542	
   543	| From   | To   | Files Added                                            |
   544	| ------ | ---- | ------------------------------------------------------ |
   545	| L1 → L2 | +Verify | `acceptance-criteria.md` + the verification region in tasks.md |
   546	| L2 → L3 | +Arch   | `decision-record.md` + addendum sections             |
   547	| L3 → L3+ | +Govern | Extended governance sections + AI protocols          |
   548	| L1 → L3 | Skip-level | Chains through L2 automatically                 |
   549	
   550	**Post-Upgrade: AI Auto-Populate (Mandatory)**
   551	
   552	After `upgrade-level.sh` runs, newly injected template sections contain `<placeholder_token>` text. The AI agent **must** auto-populate these placeholders by:
   553	
   554	1. Reading existing spec context (spec.md, plan.md, tasks.md, etc.)
   555	2. Deriving appropriate content for each placeholder from that context
   556	3. Replacing all `<placeholder_token>` markers with populated content
   557	4. Running `check-placeholders.sh` to verify none remain
   558	
   559	```bash
   560	.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/check-placeholders.sh specs/042-feature/
   561	```
   562	
   563	> **Note:** The script handles structural changes (file creation, addendum injection, backups). The AI handles semantic content — filling placeholders with project-specific information derived from existing documentation.
   564	
   565	### Manual Upgrade (Fallback)
   566	
   567	If the script is unavailable, manually add the required files:
   568	
   569	| From   | To                         | Action                                                 | Files to Add |
   570	| ------ | -------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------ | ------------ |
   571	| 1 → 2  | Add verification           | Render `templates/addons/acceptance-criteria.md.tmpl` for Level 2     |              |
   572	| 2 → 3  | Add decision documentation | Render `templates/addons/decision-record.md.tmpl` for Level 3 |              |
   573	| 3 → 3+ | Add governance             | Re-render affected manifest templates for Level 3+             |              |
   574	
   575	**Changelog example:**
   576	
   577	```markdown
   578	## Change Log
   579	- 2025-11-15: Created as Level 1 (simple feature) - spec.md, plan.md, tasks.md
   580	- 2025-11-16: Escalated to Level 2 (discovered validation needs) - added acceptance-criteria.md
   581	- 2025-11-17: Escalated to Level 3 (architectural decision required) - added decision-record.md
   582	```
   583	
   584	**Rules:**
   585	- Keep existing documentation (progressive enhancement - don't delete lower-level files)
   586	- Update `level:` field in metadata
   587	- Document reason for escalation
   588	- Inform user of level change and implications
   589	
   590	### When to Stay at Current Level
   591	
   592	**Don't escalate unnecessarily:**
   593	- Minor scope increase (50 → 95 LOC still Level 1)
   594	- Complexity didn't actually increase (just took longer than expected)
   595	- One additional file doesn't change coordination needs
   596	
   597	**Stability preferred:**
   598	- Once started, try to stay at chosen level
   599	- Only escalate if genuinely needed
   600	- Inform user before escalating
   601	
   602	---
   603	
   604	## 7. STATUS FIELD CONVENTION
   605	
   606	Every spec.md should include a status field to track lifecycle:
   607	
   608	```yaml
   609	---
   610	title: Feature Name
   611	created: 2025-11-15
   612	status: active  # ← Add this field
   613	level: 2
   614	---
   615	```
   616	
   617	### Valid Status Values
   618	
   619	| Status     | Meaning                 | When to Use                        | Reuse Priority              |
   620	| ---------- | ----------------------- | ---------------------------------- | --------------------------- |
   621	| `draft`    | Planning phase          | Initial spec creation, not started | 2 (can start)               |
   622	| `active`   | Work in progress        | Currently implementing             | 1 (highest - continue here) |
   623	| `paused`   | Temporarily on hold     | Blocked or deprioritized           | 3 (can resume)              |
   624	| `complete` | Implementation finished | Feature deployed and stable        | 4 (avoid reopening)         |
   625	| `archived` | Historical record       | Deprecated or superseded           | 5 (do not reuse)            |
   626	
   627	### Status Lifecycle
   628	
   629	```
   630	draft → active → complete → archived
   631	   ↓       ↓
   632	paused  paused
   633	   ↓
   634	active (resume)
   635	```
   636	
   637	**Update status as work progresses:**
   638	- Create spec → `draft`
   639	- Start implementation → `active`
   640	- Blocked/paused → `paused`
   641	- Deployment complete → `complete`
   642	- Feature deprecated → `archived`
   643	
   644	---
   645	
   646	## 8. RELATED SPECS: UPDATE VS CREATE
   647	
   648	### When to UPDATE Existing Spec
   649	
   650	Update an existing spec folder when:
   651	
   652	✅ **Iterative development** - Continuing work on same feature across sessions
   653	- Example: Initial implementation → bug fixes → enhancements
   654	
   655	✅ **Bug fixes** - Fixing issues in existing implementation
   656	- Example: "Fix alignment bug in markdown-c7-optimizer" → Update markdown-c7-optimizer spec
   657	
   658	✅ **Scope escalation** - Work grows beyond original estimate
   659	- Example: Level 1 bug fix → Requires Level 2 refactor → Add plan.md to same folder
   660	
   661	✅ **Feature enhancement** - Adding to existing functionality
   662	- Example: "Add dark mode to modal" → Update modal-component spec
   663	
   664	✅ **Resuming paused work** - Continuing previously paused implementation
   665	- Example: Spec status: paused → active (add continuation notes)
   666	
   667	### When to CREATE New Spec
   668	
   669	Create a new spec folder when:
   670	
   671	❌ **Distinct feature** - Completely separate functionality
   672	- Example: "markdown-c7-optimizer" ≠ "markdown-validator" (different purposes)
   673	
   674	❌ **Different approach** - Alternative implementation strategy
   675	- Example: "hero-animation-css" vs "hero-animation-js" (different approaches)
   676	
   677	❌ **Separate user story** - Different requirement or use case
   678	- Example: "user-authentication" ≠ "user-profile" (separate stories)
   679	
   680	❌ **Complete redesign** - Starting over with new architecture
   681	- Example: "payment-flow-v2" (complete rewrite of v1)
   682	
   683	❌ **Unrelated work** - No connection to existing specs
   684	- Example: "add-search-feature" ≠ "fix-form-validation" (different areas)
   685	
   686	### Decision Flowchart
   687	
   688	```
   689	User requests modification
   690	    ↓
   691	Extract keywords from request
   692	    ↓
   693	Search existing specs (folder names, titles)
   694	    ↓
   695	    ├─→ No matches found
   696	    │      ↓
   697	    │   Create new spec folder
   698	    │
   699	    └─→ Related specs found
   700	           ↓
   701	        Check status field
   702	           ↓
   703	           ├─→ status: active or draft
   704	           │      ↓
   705	           │   Recommend: UPDATE existing spec
   706	           │   Reason: Work in progress, maintain continuity
   707	           │
   708	           ├─→ status: paused
   709	           │      ↓
   710	           │   ASK user: Resume paused work or create new?
   711	           │   Reason: Context exists, but was stopped intentionally
   712	           │
   713	           └─→ status: complete or archived
   714	                  ↓
   715	               ASK user: Reopen completed work or create new?
   716	               Reason: Feature was finished, ensure not regression
   717	```
   718	
   719	---
   720	
   721	## 9. CROSS-CUTTING TEMPLATES (ANY LEVEL)
   722	
   723	Some templates are not level-specific but can be used at any documentation level. These support session management, context preservation, and work summaries.
   724	
   725	### Session Management Templates
   726	
   727	| Template | Purpose | When to Use | Created By |
   728	|----------|---------|-------------|------------|
   729	| `handover.md` | Session context transfer | End of work session requiring handoff | Main-agent narrative with `/speckit:save` handover_state routing |
   730	| `debug-delegation.md` | Debug task delegation | When stuck debugging (3+ failed attempts) | Task tool -> `@debug` |
   731	
   732	**Template Sources:**
   733	- `templates/addons/handover.md.tmpl`
   734	- `templates/addons/debug-delegation.md.tmpl`
   735	
   736	### Summary Templates (REQUIRED for ALL Levels)
   737	
   738	| Template | Purpose | When to Use | Created By |
   739	|----------|---------|-------------|------------|
   740	| `implementation-summary.md` | Post-implementation documentation | End of implementation phase (REQUIRED all levels) | Manual or context save |
   741	| `resource-map.md` | Lean path catalog | Any level, when reviewers need a scannable file ledger | Manual (optional) |
   742	
   743	**Template Sources:**
   744	- Level 1: `templates/core/implementation-summary.md.tmpl`
   745	- Level 2: `templates/core/implementation-summary.md.tmpl`
   746	- Level 3: `templates/core/implementation-summary.md.tmpl`
   747	- Level 3+: `templates/core/implementation-summary.md.tmpl`
   748	- `templates/addons/resource-map.md.tmpl`
   749	
   750	**Why Required for ALL Levels:**
   751	- `implementation-summary.md` documents outcomes, lessons learned, and deviations from plan
   752	- Ensures proper documentation trail for future sessions, handoffs, and audits
   753	- Even simple Level 1 tasks benefit from documenting what was actually done
   754	- Each level's template is progressively more detailed to match the complexity expectations
   755	
   756	### Canonical Continuity (Not Templates)
   757	
   758	| Surface | Purpose | Creation Method |
   759	|---------|---------|-----------------|
   760	| `_memory.continuity` in `implementation-summary.md` | Thin packet continuity state for resume and handoff recovery | `generate-context.js` runtime script via `/speckit:save` |
   761	| `scratch/` | Temporary workspace (disposable) | Manual creation (no template needed) |
   762	
   763	**Important:**
   764	- Canonical continuity is script-managed and should NOT be authored manually
   765	- Use `/speckit:save` or `node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/dist/continuity/generate-context.js --json '{"specFolder":"...","sessionSummary":"..."}' specs/###-folder/`
   766	- Recovery should follow `handover.md -> _memory.continuity -> spec docs`
   767	- Scratch folder contents are temporary and should be cleaned up after work completes
   768	
   769	---
   770	
   771	## 10. PHASE-AWARE SPECIFICATIONS
   772	
   773	### Phases as a Behavioral Overlay
   774	
   775	Phases are a **behavioral overlay** on the existing level system, not a new level tier. Any documentation level can technically use phases, though Level 3 and Level 3+ specifications are the most common candidates due to their inherent complexity.
   776	
   777	### How Phases Interact with Levels
   778	
   779	| Level | Phase Usage | Typical Scenario |
   780	|-------|-------------|-----------------|
   781	| Level 1 | Rare | Simple tasks rarely need decomposition |
   782	| Level 2 | Uncommon | Moderate complexity usually fits one folder |
   783	| Level 3 | Common | Complex features benefit from parallel work streams |
   784	| Level 3+ | Very common | Enterprise-scale work often requires phase decomposition |
   785	
   786	### Phase Detection Thresholds
   787	
   788	Phase decomposition is suggested when BOTH conditions are met:
   789	- Complexity score >= 25 (from 5-dimension scoring)
   790	- Documentation level >= 3
   791	
   792	A series parent is the one exception. It groups separate small packets that change the same artifact in the same track, and it qualifies without these thresholds. See `../structure/phase-definitions.md` §2.
   793	
   794	### What Phases Add to a Level
   795	
   796	Phases do not change the documentation requirements for a given level. Each phase child folder independently follows the level requirements:
   797	- A Level 3 parent with phases produces Level 3 child folders (each with spec.md, plan.md, tasks.md, acceptance-criteria.md, decision-record.md)
   798	- The parent folder adds a **Phase Documentation Map** section to its spec.md
   799	- Child folders add a **parent back-reference** to their spec.md metadata
   800	
   801	### Full Documentation
   802	
   803	See [phase-definitions.md](../structure/phase-definitions.md) for complete phase system documentation including scoring dimensions, folder structure, lifecycle, and validation rules.
   804	
   805	---
   806	
   807	## 11. RELATED RESOURCES
   808	
   809	### Reference Files
   810	- [quick-reference.md](../workflows/quick-reference.md) - Commands, checklists, and troubleshooting
   811	- [template-guide.md](./template-guide.md) - Template selection, adaptation, and quality standards
   812	- [level-selection-guide.md](./level-selection-guide.md) - 5-dimension complexity scoring and auto-detection
   813	- [path-scoped-rules.md](../validation/path-scoped-rules.md) - Path-scoped validation rules reference
   814	
   815	### Templates (Manifest-Backed Level Contracts)
   816	
   817	**Manifest Templates:**
   818	- [spec.md.tmpl](../../templates/core/spec.md.tmpl) - Essential what/why/how
   819	- [plan.md.tmpl](../../templates/core/plan.md.tmpl) - Technical approach
   820	- [tasks.md.tmpl](../../templates/core/tasks.md.tmpl) - Task breakdown
   821	- [implementation-summary.md.tmpl](../../templates/core/implementation-summary.md.tmpl) - Outcomes
   822	
   823	**Composed Level 1 (Core only ~455 LOC):**
   824	- [spec template](../../templates/core/spec.md.tmpl) - Requirements and scope
   825	- [plan.md.tmpl](../../templates/core/plan.md.tmpl) - Technical plan
   826	- [tasks.md.tmpl](../../templates/core/tasks.md.tmpl) - Task breakdown
   827	- [implementation-summary.md.tmpl](../../templates/core/implementation-summary.md.tmpl) - Outcomes
   828	
   829	**Level 2 (verification):**
   830	- All Level 1 templates + quality gates, NFRs
   831	- [acceptance-criteria.md.tmpl](../../templates/addons/acceptance-criteria.md.tmpl) - The closure gate
   832	
   833	**Level 3 (architecture):**
   834	- All Level 2 templates + architecture decisions
   835	- [decision-record.md.tmpl](../../templates/addons/decision-record.md.tmpl) - ADRs
   836	- [research.md.tmpl](../../templates/addons/research.md.tmpl) - Optional research template copied into `research/research.md`
   837	
   838	**Level 3+ (governance):**
   839	- All Level 3 templates + approval workflow, compliance, stakeholders
   840	- Extended checklist with approval tracking
   841	
   842	**Session Management Templates (Any Level):**
   843	- [handover.md.tmpl](../../templates/addons/handover.md.tmpl) - Session context transfer
   844	- [debug-delegation.md.tmpl](../../templates/addons/debug-delegation.md.tmpl) - Debug task delegation
   845	- [resource-map.md.tmpl](../../templates/addons/resource-map.md.tmpl) - Optional lean path catalog
   846	
   847	**Non-Template Helpers:**
   848	- canonical continuity inside packet docs, primarily `_memory.continuity` in `implementation-summary.md`
   849	- `scratch/` - Temporary workspace (create ad-hoc files as needed)
   850	
   851	### Related Skills
   852	- `sk-code` - Implementation, debugging, and verification lifecycle
   853	- `system-spec-kit` - Context preservation with semantic memory
   854	- `sk-git` - Git workspace setup and clean commits
   855	
   856	---

