    12	---
    13	
    14	# Level Selection Guide - Complexity Scoring Algorithm
    15	
    16	Guide to selecting appropriate documentation levels based on task complexity.
    17	
    18	---
    19	
    20	## 1. OVERVIEW
    21	
    22	> Use `--level N` with `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/create.sh` to select a level directly.
    23	
    24	The complexity detection system automatically analyzes task descriptions to:
    25	- Recommend appropriate documentation levels (1, 2, 3, or 3+)
    26	- Enable level-appropriate template features via COMPLEXITY_GATE markers
    27	- Scale section counts dynamically based on complexity
    28	
    29	**Why Level Selection Matters:**
    30	- Documentation ranges from under 60 LOC (simple) to over 3,000 LOC (complex)
    31	- Static templates cannot cover this variance
    32	- Explicit level selection ensures appropriate documentation depth
    33	
    34	---
    35	
    36	## 2. SCORING ALGORITHM
    37	
    38	`runtime/cli/spec/recommend-level.sh` scores tasks across 4 weighted factors (0-100 scale):
    39	
    40	| Factor          | Weight | What It Measures                                  |
    41	|-----------------|--------|---------------------------------------------------|
    42	| **LOC**         | 35%    | Lines-of-code estimate (0-35 points)              |
    43	| **File Count**  | 20%    | Number of files modified (0-20 points)            |
    44	| **Risk**        | 25%    | auth (+10), api (+8), db (+7) — capped at 25      |
    45	| **Complexity**  | 20%    | Architectural change (+20)                        |
    46	
    47	### Factor Details
    48	
    49	#### LOC (35%)
    50	Linear interpolation across thresholds:
    51	- 0-50 LOC: 0-8 points
    52	- 51-150 LOC: 8-18 points
    53	- 151-400 LOC: 18-28 points
    54	- 401-1000 LOC: 28-35 points
    55	- >1000 LOC: 35 points (max)
    56	
    57	#### File Count (20%)
    58	Linear interpolation across thresholds:
    59	- 1-3 files: 0-5 points
    60	- 4-8 files: 5-10 points
    61	- 9-15 files: 10-16 points
    62	- 16-30 files: 16-20 points
    63	- >30 files: 20 points (max)
    64	
    65	#### Risk (25%)
    66	Sum of risk flags, capped at 25:
    67	- `--auth` (authentication/authorization): +10
    68	- `--api` (API changes): +8
    69	- `--db` (database changes): +7
    70	
    71	#### Complexity (20%)
    72	- `--architectural` (architectural change): +20
    73	
    74	### Phase Scoring (optional, separate from level)
    75	
    76	Phase scoring uses its own 5 signals (max 50 points), enabled by default:
    77	- Architectural flag: +10
    78	- Files > 15: +10
    79	- LOC > 800: +10
    80	- Risk flags >= 2: +10
    81	- Extreme scale (Files > 30 OR LOC > 2000): +10
    82	
    83	Phases are recommended when phase score >= 25 (threshold) AND recommended level >= 3.
    84	Score 25-34 → 2 phases; 35-44 → 3 phases; 45+ → 4+ phases.
    85	
    86	A series parent is the one exception. It groups separate small packets that change the same artifact in the same track, and it qualifies without these thresholds. See `../structure/phase-definitions.md` §2.
    87	
    88	---
    89	
    90	## 3. LEVEL MAPPING
    91	
    92	| Score     | Level | Name         | Description                              |
    93	|-----------|-------|--------------|------------------------------------------|
    94	| <25       | 0     | Quick        | Trivial changes, no formal spec needed   |
    95	| 25-44     | 1     | Baseline     | Standard tasks, basic documentation      |
    96	| 45-69     | 2     | Verification | Complex tasks, full verification         |
    97	| 70+       | 3     | Full         | Critical/architectural, comprehensive    |
    98	
    99	**Level 3+ (Extended)** is an override applied on top of Level 3, not a separate score band. Coordination-root packets governing multi-phase efforts should use Level 3+ regardless of complexity score, as they require governance artifacts (phase maps, ADR records) not covered by Level 3.
   100	
   101	### Level 3+ (Extended) Features
   102	
   103	Level 3+ includes everything from Level 3 plus:
   104	- AI Execution Protocol section
   105	- Dependency graph visualization (ASCII/DAG)
   106	- Effort estimation framework
   107	- Extended checklist items (100-150 items)
   108	- Sign-off requirements
   109	
   110	---
   111	
   112	## 4. CLI TOOL
   113	
   114	### spec/create.sh
   115	
   116	Create spec folder with pre-expanded templates from level-specific folders:
   117	
   118	```bash
   119	# Create Level 1 spec folder (default)
   120	bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/create.sh "Simple bugfix"
   121	
   122	# Create Level 2 spec folder
   123	bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/create.sh "Add OAuth2 authentication" --level 2
   124	
   125	# Create Level 3 spec folder
   126	bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/create.sh "Major architecture redesign" --level 3
   127	
   128	# Create Level 3+ spec folder (extended)
   129	bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/create.sh "Platform migration" --level 3+
   130	```
   131	
   132	**Template Source:**
   133	Templates are Level-gated manifest files rendered by `create.sh`:
   134	- Level 1 - spec.md, plan.md, tasks.md, implementation-summary.md
   135	- Level 2 - Level 1 plus acceptance-criteria.md
   136	- Level 3 - Level 2 plus decision-record.md
   137	- Level 3+ - Level 3 file set with governance sections rendered by gate
   138	
   139	> **Source of truth for template LOC counts:** [level-specifications.md](./level-specifications.md)
   140	
   141	---
   142	
   143	## 5. DYNAMIC SECTION SCALING
   144	
   145	Sections scale based on detected complexity:
   146	
   147	| Section | Level 1 | Level 2 | Level 3 | Level 3+ |
   148	|---------|---------|---------|---------|----------|
   149	| User Stories (spec.md) | 1-2 | 2-4 | 4-8 | 8-15 |
   150	| Phases (plan.md) | 2-3 | 3-5 | 5-8 | 8-12 |
   151	| Tasks (tasks.md) | 5-15 | 15-50 | 50-100+ | 100+ |
   152	| Checklist Items | 10-20 | 30-50 | 60-100 | 100-150 |
   153	| AI Protocol | None | None | Optional | Required |
   154	| Dependency Graph | None | Table | ASCII | Full DAG |
   155	
   156	---
   157	
   158	## 6. VALIDATION RULES
   159	
   160	Four validation rules ensure complexity consistency:
   161	
   162	### check-complexity.sh
   163	Validates declared level matches actual content metrics:
   164	- User story count within expected range
   165	- Phase count within expected range
   166	- Task count within expected range
   167	- AI protocol present for Level 3+
   168	
   169	### check-ai-protocols.sh
   170	For Level 3+ specs, validates AI protocol components:
   171	- Pre-Task Checklist present
   172	- Execution Rules table present
   173	- Status Reporting Format present
   174	- Blocked Task Protocol present
   175	
   176	### check-level-match.sh
   177	Validates level consistency across all spec files:
   178	- spec.md, plan.md, acceptance-criteria.md declare same level
   179	- Required files exist for declared level
   180	
   181	### Running Validation
   182	
   183	```bash
   184	# Run all complexity validation rules
   185	bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/rules/check-complexity.sh specs/XXX/
   186	bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/rules/check-ai-protocols.sh specs/XXX/
   187	bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/rules/check-level-match.sh specs/XXX/
   188	
   189	# Exit codes:
   190	# 0 = PASS
   191	# 1 = WARN (non-blocking)
   192	# 2 = FAIL (blocking)
   193	```
   194	
   195	---
   196	
   197	## 7. RELATED RESOURCES
   198	
   199	### Templates (Manifest-Based Organization)
   200	
   201	Templates are selected from `templates/spec-kit-docs.json`:
   202	
   203	| Level | Source | Rendered Content |
   204	|-------|--------|---------------------|
   205	| Level 1 | `templates/spec-kit-docs.json` | Baseline templates (spec, plan, tasks, implementation-summary) |
   206	| Level 2 | `templates/spec-kit-docs.json` | Level 1 + acceptance-criteria.md |
   207	| Level 3 | `templates/spec-kit-docs.json` | Level 2 file set with architecture sections; add-ons stay lazy |
   208	| Level 3+ | `templates/spec-kit-docs.json` | Level 3 file set with governance sections |
   209	
   210	**Example paths:**
   211	- `templates/core/spec.md.tmpl` - Level-gated spec template
   212	- `templates/addons/acceptance-criteria.md.tmpl` - The closure-gate document scaffolded at Level 2 and above
   213	- `templates/addons/decision-record.md.tmpl` - Level-gated ADR template
   214	- `templates/core/spec.md.tmpl` - Level 3+ spec sections are rendered by gate
   215	
   216	### Reference Documentation
   217	- [level-specifications.md](./level-specifications.md) - Complete level requirements
   218	- [template-guide.md](./template-guide.md) - Template selection and adaptation
   219	
   220	---

