    12	---
    13	
    14	# Template Guide - Template Selection, Adaptation & Quality Standards
    15	
    16	Guide to template selection, copying, adaptation, and quality standards.
    17	
    18	---
    19	
    20	## 1. OVERVIEW
    21	
    22	### Progressive Enhancement Model
    23	
    24	```text
    25	Level 1 (Baseline):     spec.md + plan.md + tasks.md + implementation-summary.md
    26	                               ↓
    27	Level 2 (Verification): Level 1 + acceptance-criteria.md
    28	                               ↓
    29	Level 3 (Full):         Level 2 file set + architecture sections; every add-on stays lazy
    30	                               ↓
    31	Level 3+ (Extended):    Level 3 + governance/AI protocol content
    32	```
    33	
    34	### Core Principles
    35	
    36	1. **Never create from scratch** - Always scaffold through `create.sh` or render from `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/templates/`
    37	2. **Always adapt to feature** - Templates are starting points, not final documents
    38	3. **Preserve structure** - Preserve section structure and numbering; emojis are optional
    39	4. **Remove placeholders** - Replace ALL `[PLACEHOLDER]` text with actual content
    40	5. **Delete samples** - Remove `<!-- SAMPLE CONTENT -->` blocks before delivery
    41	
    42	### Why Templates Matter
    43	
    44	**Without templates:**
    45	- Inconsistent documentation structure
    46	- Missing critical sections
    47	- Ad-hoc format that's hard to navigate
    48	- Wasted time deciding what to document
    49	
    50	**With templates:**
    51	- Consistent structure across all specs
    52	- Complete coverage (no forgotten sections)
    53	- Easy to scan and find information
    54	- Faster documentation creation
    55	
    56	### Template Composition (Maintainer Reference)
    57	
    58	Templates are rendered from manifest-backed `*.md.tmpl` files. The same Level contract drives scaffolding and validation:
    59	
    60	```bash
    61	bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/templates/inline-gate-renderer.sh \
    62	  --level 3 \
    63	  --out-dir /tmp/spec-kit-render \
    64	  .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/templates/core/spec.md.tmpl \
    65	  .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/templates/core/plan.md.tmpl
    66	```
    67	
    68	**Composition Rules:**
    69	- Level 1: baseline sections only
    70	- Level 2: baseline + verification sections
    71	- Level 3: Level 2 + architecture sections
    72	- Level 3+: Level 3 + governance sections
    73	- Nested packet changelog templates live in `templates/changelog/` and are consumed directly by the generator, not by the composition pipeline.
    74	
    75	> **Note:** Maintainers should use `inline-gate-renderer --level N --out-dir DIR file...` after modifying manifest templates to inspect rendered output before running validation tests.
    76	
    77	### Reading a Template (Agents)
    78	
    79	**Why render, not open:** A raw manifest `*.md.tmpl` is the full ungated file (hundreds of lines). Rendering it to a level yields only that level's content — much smaller and exactly what the agent needs. Reading the raw file re-introduces the token wall the level gate exists to avoid.
    80	
    81	```bash
    82	bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/templates/inline-gate-renderer.sh --level <1|2|3|3+|phase> <path-to-template.md.tmpl>
    83	```
    84	
    85	Omit `--out-dir` to print the level-rendered content to STDOUT. Do not open raw `*.md.tmpl` files to read their content; render them to the target level instead.
    86	
    87	---
    88	
    89	## 2. TEMPLATE SELECTION BY LEVEL (PROGRESSIVE ENHANCEMENT)
    90	
    91	### Level 1: Baseline Documentation (LOC guidance: <100)
    92	
    93	**Required Templates:** `spec.md` + `plan.md` + `tasks.md` + `implementation-summary.md`
    94	
    95	**Optional completion artifact:** packet-local changelog via `templates/changelog/root.md` when a durable spec-local history is useful
    96	
    97	**Scaffold command:**
    98	```bash
    99	bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/create.sh --level 1 --path specs/###-name --name feature-name
   100	```
   101	
   102	**When to use:**
   103	- **All features start here** - this is the minimum documentation
   104	- <100 LOC, as one input to `recommend-level.sh`
   105	- Localized to one component or trivial changes
   106	- Clear requirements
   107	- Low to moderate complexity
   108	
   109	**spec.md sections to fill:**
   110	- Metadata block (created, status, level, LOC estimate)
   111	- Problem statement
   112	- Proposed solution
   113	- Files to change
   114	- Testing approach
   115	- Success criteria
   116	
   117	**plan.md sections to fill:**
   118	- Implementation approach
   119	- File changes breakdown
   120	- Testing strategy
   121	- Dependencies
   122	
   123	**tasks.md sections to fill:**
   124	- Task breakdown by user story
   125	- Dependencies between tasks
   126	- Estimated effort per task
   127	
   128	**Adaptation tips:**
   129	- Keep problem statement focused and specific
   130	- For trivial changes (typos, single-line fixes), keep spec concise
   131	- Proposed solution should be clear and actionable
   132	- List specific files (not just "various files")
   133	- Testing approach should match scope (unit tests? manual verification?)
   134	- Success criteria should be measurable
   135	
   136	**Enforcement:** Hard block if any required file missing
   137	
   138	---
   139	
   140	### Level 2: Verification Added (LOC guidance: 100-499)
   141	
   142	**Required Templates:** Level 1 + `acceptance-criteria.md`
   143	
   144	**Scaffold command:**
   145	```bash
   146	bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/create.sh --level 2 --path specs/###-name --name feature-name
   147	```
   148	
   149	**When to use:**
   150	- Features needing systematic QA validation
   151	- 100-499 LOC, as one input to `recommend-level.sh`
   152	- Multiple files/components
   153	- Moderate complexity
   154	- High risk areas (security, config cascades)
   155	
   156	**All Level 1 sections PLUS:**
   157	
   158	**acceptance-criteria.md sections to fill:**
   159	- Pre-implementation checks
   160	- Implementation validation
   161	- Testing checklist
   162	- Deployment verification
   163	- Security checks (if applicable)
   164	
   165	**Adaptation tips:**
   166	- All Level 1 tips apply
   167	- Make checklist specific to feature (not generic)
   168	- Include edge cases and error scenarios
   169	- Add security checks if relevant
   170	- Include rollback verification
   171	
   172	**Enforcement:** `AC_CLOSURE` fails when `acceptance-criteria.md` is absent from a packet created after 2026-08-30; older packets stay advisory
   173	
   174	---
   175	
   176	### Level 3: Full Documentation (LOC guidance: >=500)
   177	
   178	**Templates:** the Level 2 set; `decision-record.md` is a lazy add-on here as at every level
   179	
   180	**Recommended completion artifact for phased work:** packet-local changelog files generated from `templates/changelog/root.md` or `templates/changelog/phase.md`
   181	
   182	**Scaffold command:**
   183	```bash
   184	bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/create.sh --level 3 --path specs/###-name --name feature-name
   185	```
   186	
   187	Add `--with-lazy-addons` to scaffold `before-after.md`, `timeline.md`, `roadmap.md` and `decision-record.md`, and `--with-goal` to scaffold `goal.md`. Both work at every level, but with `--phase` the goal flag writes a `goal.md` into each child only. Author a parent goal, or any goal after scaffolding, with `/create:goal`.
   188	
   189	**Optional Templates:**
   190	```bash
   191	mkdir -p specs/###-name/research
   192	bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/templates/inline-gate-renderer.sh \
   193	  --level 3 \
   194	  --out-dir specs/###-name/research \
   195	  .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/templates/addons/research.md.tmpl
   196	
   197	bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/templates/inline-gate-renderer.sh \
   198	  --level 3 \
   199	  --out-dir specs/###-name \
   200	  .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/templates/addons/resource-map.md.tmpl
   201	```
   202	
   203	**When to use:**
   204	- Complex features, architecture changes, major decisions
   205	- >=500 LOC, as one input to `recommend-level.sh`
   206	- Multiple systems involved
   207	- Significant architectural impact
   208	- Major technical decisions need documentation
   209	
   210	**All Level 2 sections PLUS:**
   211	
   212	**decision-record.md sections to fill:**
   213	- Context and problem
   214	- Options considered (2-4 typically)
   215	- Decision made
   216	- Rationale
   217	- Consequences and trade-offs
   218	
   219	**Adaptation tips:**
   220	- All Level 2 tips apply
   221	- Present 2-4 viable options (not every possible choice)
   222	- Fair comparison (pros/cons for each)
   223	- Clear decision with rationale
   224	- Document trade-offs honestly
   225	- Note what was sacrificed for chosen path
   226	
   227	**Enforcement:** none. `decision-record.md` is a lazy add-on at every level; the level-match rule only warns when one appears in a Level 1 packet
   228	
   229	---
   230	
   231	### Level 3+: Extended Governance (Complexity score: 80-100)
   232	
   233	**Required Templates:** Same file set as Level 3, rendered at Level 3+.
   234	
   235	**Scaffold command:**
   236	```bash
   237	bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/create.sh --level 3+ --path specs/###-name --name feature-name
   238	```
   239	
   240	**When to use:**
   241	- Multi-agent or multi-workstream coordination
   242	- High-risk architectural or governance-heavy work
   243	- Compliance requirements and formal approval tracking
   244	
   245	**Level 3+ expectations:**
   246	- AI execution protocol sections remain intact and project-specific
   247	- Extended checklist includes P0/P1/P2 gating and approval tracking sections
   248	- Governance, approval, and stakeholder communication sections are populated
   249	
   250	**Enforcement:** Hard block if required Level 3 files are missing; quality gates rely on checklist completion and validation scripts
   251	
   252	**If spec is a coordination-root packet**: Replace the executive summary with a coordination overview. Include point-in-time snapshot metadata, a direct-phase documentation map, and cross-reference the governing ADR. Skip dependency graphs in favor of phase navigation links.
   253	
   254	---
   255	
   256	## 3. TEMPLATE STRUCTURE STANDARDS
   257	
   258	### 1. Numbered H2 Sections
   259	
   260	**Format:** `## N. EMOJI TITLE`
   261	
   262	**Example:**
   263	```markdown
   264	\## 1. OBJECTIVE
   265	\## 2. SCOPE
   266	\## 3. IMPLEMENTATION
   267	```
   268	
   269	**Rules:**
   270	- Keep numbering sequential (1, 2, 3, ...)
   271	- Never remove emojis (they provide visual scanning cues)
   272	- Use ALL CAPS for section titles
   273	- Maintain consistent spacing
   274	
   275	### 2. Metadata Block
   276	
   277	**Level 1 metadata:**
   278	```markdown
   279	---
   280	created: 2025-11-23
   281	status: active
   282	level: 1
   283	estimated_loc: 85
   284	complexity: low
   285	---
   286	```
   287	
   288	**Level 2/3 metadata:**
   289	```markdown
   290	---
   291	title: Feature Name
   292	category: Implementation
   293	tags: [feature, authentication, security]
   294	priority: P1
   295	status: active
   296	created: 2025-11-23
   297	level: 2
   298	estimated_loc: 350
   299	---
   300	```
   301	
   302	### 3. Placeholder Conventions
   303	
   304	**Placeholder types:**
   305	
   306	- `[PLACEHOLDER]` - **MUST** be replaced with actual content
   307	- `[NEEDS CLARIFICATION: ...]` - Unknown requirement (flag for user)
   308	- `<!-- SAMPLE CONTENT -->` - Remove before delivery
   309	
   310	**Example before adaptation:**
   311	```markdown
   312	## Problem
   313	
   314	[PLACEHOLDER: Describe the problem this solves]
   315	
   316	<!-- SAMPLE CONTENT
   317	The form submission button doesn't show loading state, causing users to
   318	double-click and submit forms multiple times.
   319	-->
   320	```
   321	
   322	**Example after adaptation:**
   323	```markdown
   324	## Problem
   325	
   326	The form submission button doesn't show loading state, causing users to
   327	double-click and submit forms multiple times. Analytics show 23% of form
   328	submissions are duplicates.
   329	```
   330	
   331	### 4. Template Footer
   332	
   333	**Appears at bottom of template:**
   334	```html
   335	<!--
   336	  REPLACE SAMPLE CONTENT IN FINAL OUTPUT
   337	  - This template contains placeholders and examples
   338	  - Replace them with actual content
   339	  - Remove this footer before delivery
   340	-->
   341	```
   342	
   343	**Action:** Delete this footer after filling template
   344	
   345	---
   346	
   347	## 4. TEMPLATE ADAPTATION PROCESS
   348	
   349	### Step-by-Step Adaptation
   350	
   351	**Step 1: Scaffold or Render Template**
   352	```bash
   353	bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/create.sh --level <N> --path specs/###-name --name feature-name
   354	# Where <N> is 1, 2, 3, or 3+ matching your documentation level
   355	```
   356	
   357	**Step 2: Fill Metadata Block**
   358	- Set created date (today)
   359	- Set status (usually "draft" or "active")
   360	- Set level (1/2/3/3+)
   361	- Set estimated LOC
   362	- Add other metadata as needed
   363	
   364	**Step 3: Replace ALL Placeholders**
   365	- Search for `[PLACEHOLDER]`
   366	- Replace with actual content
   367	- Verify no placeholders remain
   368	
   369	**Step 4: Review Sample Content**
   370	- Search for `<!-- SAMPLE CONTENT -->`
   371	- Read sample for context
   372	- Write actual content
   373	- Delete sample blocks
   374	
   375	**Step 5: Fill All Sections**
   376	- Go section by section
   377	- Replace generic text with specifics
   378	- Keep sections relevant (use "N/A" if not applicable)
   379	- Don't delete sections (maintain structure)
   380	
   381	**Step 6: Cross-Reference Sibling Docs**
   382	- Link spec.md ↔ plan.md
   383	- Link plan.md ↔ tasks.md
   384	- Link to decision records and research documents
   385	- Create navigation between documents
   386	
   387	**Step 7: Delete Instructional Content**
   388	- Remove template footer
   389	- Remove instructional comments
   390	- Remove sample blocks
   391	- Keep only actual content
   392	
   393	**Step 8: Final Review**
   394	- No placeholders remain
   395	- All sections filled with actual content
   396	- Cross-references working
   397	- Metadata accurate
   398	- Structure preserved
   399	
   400	---
   401	
   402	## 5. SUPPORTING TEMPLATES
   403	
   404	### research/research.md - Comprehensive Feature Research
   405	
   406	**When to use:** Before implementation for complex features requiring deep technical investigation
   407	
   408	**Purpose:** Comprehensive research documentation spanning multiple technical areas
   409	
   410	**Template:** `research/research.md`
   411	
   412	**Copy command:**
   413	```bash
   414	mkdir -p specs/###-name/research
   415	bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/templates/inline-gate-renderer.sh \
   416	  --level 3 \
   417	  --out-dir specs/###-name/research \
   418	  .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/templates/addons/research.md.tmpl
   419	```
   420	
   421	**Sections to fill:**
   422	- Investigation report (request summary, current behavior, findings, recommendations)
   423	- Executive overview (summary, architecture diagram, quick reference)
   424	- Core architecture (components, data flow, integration points, dependencies)
   425	- Technical specifications (API docs, attributes, events, state management)
   426	- Constraints & limitations (platform, security, performance, compatibility)
   427	- Integration patterns (third-party services, auth, error handling, retries)
   428	- Implementation guide (markup, JavaScript, CSS, configuration)
   429	- Code examples & snippets (initialization, helpers, API usage, edge cases)
   430	- Testing & debugging (strategies, E2E examples, diagnostic tools)
   431	- Performance optimization (tactics, benchmarks, caching)
   432	- Security considerations (validation, data protection, auth)
   433	- Maintenance & future-proofing (upgrades, compatibility, SPA support)
   434	- API reference (attributes table, methods, events, cleanup)
   435	- Troubleshooting guide (common issues, error messages, solutions)
   436	
   437	**Adaptation tips:**
   438	- Use for larger research efforts spanning multiple areas
   439	- Serves as authoritative reference during implementation
   440	- Remove N/A sections or mark clearly as not applicable
   441	- Consider creating decision-record-*.md for significant technical choices discovered during research
   442	
   443	---
   444	
   445	### tasks.md - Task Breakdown
   446	
   447	**When to use:** After plan.md, before coding
   448	
   449	**Purpose:** Break implementation plan into actionable tasks
   450	
   451	**Template:** `tasks.md`
   452	
   453	**Copy command:**
   454	```bash
   455	bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/templates/inline-gate-renderer.sh \
   456	  --level 1 \
   457	  --out-dir specs/###-name \
   458	  .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/templates/core/tasks.md.tmpl
   459	# Or render with --level 3 when the packet is Level 3
   460	```
   461	
   462	**Sections to fill:**
   463	- Task list (ordered by dependencies)
   464	- Estimated effort per task
   465	- Dependencies between tasks
   466	- Task ownership (if multi-person)
   467	
   468	**Adaptation tips:**
   469	- Each task should be completable in <1 day
   470	- Clear dependencies (Task 2 requires Task 1)
   471	- Specific and actionable ("Add email validation" not "Fix form")
   472	
   473	---
   474	
   475	### acceptance-criteria.md - Closure Criteria
   476	
   477	**When to use:** When systematic validation needed
   478	
   479	**Purpose:** QA steps, deployment checks, security review
   480	
   481	**Template:** `acceptance-criteria.md`
   482	
   483	**Copy command:**
   484	```bash
   485	bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/templates/inline-gate-renderer.sh \
   486	  --level 2 \
   487	  --out-dir specs/###-name \
   488	  .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/templates/addons/acceptance-criteria.md.tmpl
   489	# Or render with --level 3+ when the packet is Level 3+
   490	```
   491	
   492	**Sections to fill:**
   493	- Pre-implementation checks
   494	- Implementation validation
   495	- Testing checklist
   496	- Deployment verification
   497	
   498	**Adaptation tips:**
   499	- Make checklist specific to feature (not generic)
   500	- Include edge cases and error scenarios
   501	- Add security checks if relevant
   502	- Include rollback verification
   503	
   504	---
   505	
   506	### decision-record.md - Architecture Decision Record (ADR)
   507	
   508	**When to use:** Major technical decisions
   509	
   510	**Purpose:** Document significant choices with rationale
   511	
   512	**Template:** `decision-record.md`
   513	
   514	**Copy command:**
   515	```bash
   516	bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/templates/inline-gate-renderer.sh \
   517	  --level 3 \
   518	  --out-dir specs/###-name \
   519	  .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/templates/addons/decision-record.md.tmpl
   520	```
   521	
   522	**Required filename:** `decision-record.md` (topic-specific ADR files are supplemental)
   523	
   524	**Sections to fill:**
   525	- Context and problem
   526	- Options considered (2-4 typically)
   527	- Decision made
   528	- Rationale
   529	- Consequences and trade-offs
   530	
   531	**Adaptation tips:**
   532	- Present 2-4 viable options (not every possible choice)
   533	- Fair comparison (pros/cons for each)
   534	- Clear decision with rationale
   535	- Document trade-offs honestly
   536	- Note what was sacrificed for chosen path
   537	
   538	**When to create:**
   539	- Database or framework choice
   540	- Architectural pattern selection
   541	- Library/tool selection
   542	- Infrastructure/deployment strategy
   543	- Major refactoring approach
   544	
   545	---
   546	
   547	## 6. SESSION MANAGEMENT TEMPLATES
   548	
   549	These templates support session continuity, temporary workspaces, and context preservation.
   550	
   551	### handover.md - Full Session Handover
   552	
   553	**When to use:** End of complex work sessions requiring comprehensive context transfer
   554	
   555	**Purpose:** Comprehensive session handover document for complex, multi-phase work
   556	
   557	**Created by:** Main-agent narrative plus `/speckit:save` handover_state routing (initial file seeded from the template)
   558	
   559	**Location:** Spec folder root
   560	
   561	**Copy command:**
   562	```bash
   563	bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/templates/inline-gate-renderer.sh \
   564	  --level 3 \
   565	  --out-dir specs/###-name \
   566	  .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/templates/addons/handover.md.tmpl
   567	```
   568	
   569	**Sections to fill:**
   570	- Session summary (what was accomplished)
   571	- Current state (where things stand)
   572	- Key decisions made (with rationale)
   573	- Blockers and open questions
   574	- Next steps (prioritized)
   575	- Files modified (with change descriptions)
   576	- Context needed for continuation
   577	
   578	**Adaptation tips:**
   579	- Be thorough - future sessions depend on this context
   580	- Document decisions even if they seem obvious now
   581	- List specific blockers with attempted solutions
   582	- Prioritize next steps (what's most important first)
   583	- Include links to relevant files and line numbers
   584	
   585	**Size:** ~100-150 lines (comprehensive)
   586	
   587	---
   588	
   589	### Scratch Folder (Not a Template)
   590	
   591	The `scratch/` folder is a directory for temporary, disposable files - NOT a template file.
   592	
   593	**Usage:**
   594	- Place debug logs, test scripts, prototypes in `scratch/`
   595	- Files in scratch/ are gitignored and disposable
   596	- Clean up scratch/ contents when done
   597	
   598	**Location:** `specs/###-name/scratch/` subfolder (NEVER in spec root)
   599	
   600	**Creation:**
   601	```bash
   602	mkdir -p specs/###-name/scratch
   603	# Create ad-hoc files as needed - no formal template
   604	```
   605	
   606	**Example contents (informal, ad-hoc):**
   607	- Debug output and logs
   608	- Test results (temporary)
   609	- Ideas and notes to self
   610	- Command history
   611	- Temporary findings
   612	
   613	**Note:** There is no `scratch-notes.md` template. Create ad-hoc files in the scratch/ folder as needed.
   614	
   615	**Cleanup rule:** Delete or archive `scratch/` contents when task completes. Move valuable findings into canonical packet docs or `handover.md` before cleanup.
   616	
   617	---
   618	
   619	### Canonical Continuity Saves (Script-Managed)
   620	
   621	Continuity is no longer a standalone `memory/*.md` template surface. `generate-context.js` updates the target packet's canonical continuity surfaces, centered on `_memory.continuity` in `implementation-summary.md`. Recovery reads those docs directly; nothing is re-indexed and no database marker is touched.
   622	
   623	**Creation (JSON mode — preferred):** `node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/dist/continuity/generate-context.js --json '{"specFolder":"###-name","sessionSummary":"..."}' specs/###-name/`
   624	
   625	**Primary surface:** `_memory.continuity` frontmatter in `implementation-summary.md`
   626	
   627	**Example creation:**
   628	```bash
   629	node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/dist/continuity/generate-context.js /tmp/save-context-data-<session-id>.json specs/007-feature
   630	```
   631	
   632	**Typical continuity fields:**
   633	- `packet_pointer`
   634	- `last_updated_at`
   635	- `last_updated_by`
   636	- `recent_action`
   637	- `next_safe_action`
   638	- `key_files`
   639	
   640	**IMPORTANT:**
   641	- **NEVER author continuity surfaces manually** - always use `generate-context.js` via the runtime `node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/dist/continuity/generate-context.js` entrypoint
   642	- The save path is packet-first: recovery should start with `handover.md -> _memory.continuity -> spec docs`
   643	- Manual editing bypasses the routed save/index flow and makes recovery evidence less trustworthy
   644	
   645	---
