    13	
    14	# Quick Reference - Commands, Checklists & Troubleshooting
    15	
    16	> Setting a packet's session goal? The shape of what you type — a pointer plus the
    17	> completion criteria copied out — is in [goal-set-string-playbook.md](./goal-set-string-playbook.md).
    18	> Writing or amending a `goal.md`, including a phase parent's binding table? Use
    19	> `/create:goal` from `sk-create-goal`.
    20	
    21	Fast lookup for spec folder commands, checklists, and troubleshooting.
    22	
    23	---
    24	
    25	## 1. OVERVIEW
    26	
    27	### Purpose
    28	
    29	Quick reference for spec folder management, commands, and troubleshooting using the progressive enhancement model.
    30	
    31	### Progressive Enhancement Model
    32	
    33	```
    34	Level 1 (Baseline):     spec.md + plan.md + tasks.md + implementation-summary.md
    35	Level 2 (Verification): Level 1 + acceptance-criteria.md
    36	Level 3 (Full):         Level 2 + decision-record.md + optional research
    37	Level 3+ (Extended):    Level 3 + governance/AI execution content
    38	```
    39	
    40	### Key Points
    41	
    42	- LOC thresholds are **SOFT GUIDANCE** (not enforcement)
    43	- **Enforcement is MANUAL** - verify required templates exist before claiming completion
    44	
    45	---
    46	
    47	## 2. LEVEL DECISION SHORTCUTS (PROGRESSIVE ENHANCEMENT)
    48	
    49	| Situation | Level | Required Templates | Optional Templates |
    50	|-----------|-------|-------------------|-------------------|
    51	| Any task (baseline) | 1 | spec.md + plan.md + tasks.md + implementation-summary.md | resource-map.md (any level) |
    52	| Needs QA validation | 2 | L1 + acceptance-criteria.md | resource-map.md (any level) |
    53	| Complex/architectural | 3 | L2 + decision-record.md | research/research.md, resource-map.md |
    54	| Enterprise/governance heavy | 3+ | L3 file set rendered at Level 3+ | research/research.md, resource-map.md |
    55	
    56	**LOC as one input to `runtime/cli/spec/recommend-level.sh`, which scores it with file count and risk:**
    57	- <100 LOC suggests Level 1
    58	- 100-499 LOC suggests Level 2
    59	- >=500 LOC suggests Level 3
    60	- High complexity/risk and governance needs suggest Level 3+
    61	
    62	---
    63	
    64	## 3. TEMPLATE COPY COMMANDS (PROGRESSIVE)
    65	
    66	### Level 1: Baseline (ALL features start here)
    67	
    68	```bash
    69	bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/create.sh --level 1 --path specs/###-name --name feature-name
    70	```
    71	
    72	### Level 2: Verification (complete set)
    73	
    74	```bash
    75	bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/create.sh --level 2 --path specs/###-name --name feature-name
    76	```
    77	
    78	### Level 3: Full Documentation (complete set)
    79	
    80	```bash
    81	bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/create.sh --level 3 --path specs/###-name --name feature-name
    82	```
    83	
    84	### Level 3+: Extended Documentation (complete set)
    85	
    86	```bash
    87	bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/create.sh --level 3+ --path specs/###-name --name feature-name
    88	```
    89	
    90	### Optional Templates (Level 3 Only)
    91	
    92	```bash
    93	## Comprehensive Research (from root templates folder):
    94	bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/templates/inline-gate-renderer.sh \
    95	  --level 3 \
    96	  --out-dir specs/###-name/research \
    97	  .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/templates/addons/research.md.tmpl
    98	```
    99	
   100	---
   101	
   102	## 4. ESSENTIAL COMMANDS
   103	
   104	### Start Here: Command Map
   105	
   106	If the runtime does not surface command menus clearly, use this compact command map first.
   107	
   108	**SpecKit workflow commands**
   109	- `/speckit:resume` - continue or recover existing spec-folder work
   110	- `/speckit:plan` - define or refine a scoped spec and plan
   111	- `/speckit:implement` - execute an existing spec and plan packet
   112	- `/speckit:complete` - run the full spec-to-implementation lifecycle
   113	
   114	**Deep-loop workflow commands**
   115	- `/deep:research` - run iterative research before planning
   116	- `/deep:review` - run iterative review and finding synthesis
   117	- `/deep:ai-council` - run multi-seat planning deliberation
   118	
   119	**Memory commands**
   120	- `/speckit:save` - write the current session context into the packet's continuity documents
   121	- `/speckit:search` - retrieve prior context, decisions, and analysis by ripgrep over spec and skill docs
   122	
   123	**Nested changelog generator**
   124	- `node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/dist/spec-folder/nested-changelog.js <spec-folder> --write` - publish a packet-local changelog for a root spec or phase child
   125	
   126	### Create Spec Folder
   127	
   128	Use `create.sh`. It numbers the packet from the highest number in use, and before it does, it lists the recent packets in the same track:
   129	
   130	```bash
   131	bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/create.sh --track <track> --level <N> "<short description>"
   132	```
   133	
   134	### Template Composition (Maintainer)
   135	
   136	```bash
   137	# Render one or more manifest templates for inspection
   138	bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/templates/inline-gate-renderer.sh \
   139	  --level 3 \
   140	  --out-dir /tmp/spec-kit-render \
   141	  .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/templates/core/spec.md.tmpl \
   142	  .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/templates/core/plan.md.tmpl
   143	```
   144	
   145	`create.sh --path` validates the resolved target before writing and rejects traversal outside the repository. Post-create validation is opt-in: set `SPECKIT_POST_VALIDATE=1` when CI or a strict local workflow should run `validate.sh --quiet` immediately after scaffolding.
   146	
   147	**Naming rules:**
   148	- 2-3 words (shorter is better)
   149	- Lowercase
   150	- Hyphen-separated
   151	- Action-noun structure
   152	
   153	**Good examples:** `fix-typo`, `add-auth`, `mcp-code-mode`, `cli-opencode`
   154	
   155	### Manual Context Save
   156	
   157	Trigger manual context save:
   158	```
   159	Say: "save context" or "save conversation"
   160	```
   161	
   162	**Required Inputs:** Structured JSON is mandatory for routine saves, and the target spec folder is still required
   163	```
   164	node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/dist/continuity/generate-context.js /tmp/save-context-data-<session-id>.json specs/007-feature/
   165	```
   166	
   167	Use `generate-context.js` for routine metadata saves. Quick direct edits are allowed only for `_memory.continuity` YAML frontmatter blocks in `implementation-summary.md`. There is no indexing hand-off after a save; regenerate `runtime/data/trigger-index.json` only when a document's `trigger_phrases` changed.
   168	
   169	---
   170	
   171	## 5. PRE-IMPLEMENTATION CHECKLIST (PROGRESSIVE ENHANCEMENT)
   172	
   173	Before making ANY file changes, verify:
   174	
   175	- [ ] Determined level (1/2/3/3+) or exempt (typo fix)
   176	- [ ] Created `/specs/[###-short-name]/`
   177	- [ ] Scaffolded ALL REQUIRED templates for chosen level:
   178	  - [ ] Level 1: spec.md + plan.md + tasks.md + implementation-summary.md
   179	  - [ ] Level 2: Level 1 + acceptance-criteria.md
   180	  - [ ] Level 3: Level 2 + decision-record.md
   181	  - [ ] Level 3+: Use full Level 3 file set rendered at Level 3+
   182	- [ ] Rendered template output names are correct
   183	- [ ] Filled ALL template sections with actual content
   184	- [ ] Removed placeholder text and sample sections
   185	- [ ] Copied optional templates if needed (Level 3 only)
   186	- [ ] Presented approach to user (including templates used)
   187	- [ ] Got explicit approval ("yes"/"go ahead"/"proceed")
   188	
   189	**If ANY unchecked → STOP**
   190	**If required template missing → Cannot claim completion**
   191	
   192	---
   193	
   194	## 6. FOLDER NAMING EXAMPLES
   195	
   196	### Good Examples ✅
   197	
   198	- `fix-typo` (concise, clear)
   199	- `add-validation` (action-noun)
   200	- `implement-auth` (descriptive)
   201	- `cdn-migration` (noun-noun acceptable)
   202	- `hero-animation-v2` (version included)
   203	
   204	### Bad Examples ❌
   205	
   206	- `fix-the-typo-in-header-component` (too long - max 4 words)
   207	- `fixTypo` (not kebab-case)
   208	- `fix_typo` (snake_case, should be kebab-case)
   209	- `typo` (too vague, lacks context)
   210	- `PROJ-123-fix` (no ticket numbers)
   211	
   212	---
   213	
   214	## 7. STATUS FIELD VALUES
   215	
   216	| Status | Meaning | Reuse Priority |
   217	|--------|---------|----------------|
   218	| `draft` | Planning phase | 2 (can start) |
   219	| `active` | Work in progress | 1 (highest - continue here) |
   220	| `paused` | Temporarily on hold | 3 (can resume) |
   221	| `complete` | Implementation finished | 4 (avoid reopening) |
   222	| `archived` | Historical record | 5 (do not reuse) |
   223	
   224	---
   225	
   226	## 8. UPDATE, PHASE, OR CREATE DECISION
   227	
   228	### CONTINUE or UPDATE Existing Work When:
   229	
   230	✅ Iterative development of the same feature
   231	✅ Bug fixes to the existing implementation
   232	✅ Scope escalation within the same outcome
   233	✅ Enhancement of existing functionality
   234	✅ Resuming paused work
   235	✅ The request fits the active packet's purpose, scope, requirements, or Phase Documentation Map
   236	
   237	### PREFER A PHASED PACKET When:
   238	
   239	✅ The work is related to one coordinated outcome
   240	✅ Phase complexity score >= 25 AND documentation level >= 3
   241	✅ The work contains distinct workstreams that would otherwise become separate top-level single-spec folders
   242	✅ The work is a different change to the same artifact as an existing packet in the same track (a series parent, `phase-definitions.md` §2)
   243	
   244	The phased-packet preference does not override qualification. If only one or neither threshold is met and no series parent qualifies, continue or update a standard non-phased packet.
   245	
   246	### CREATE New Top-Level Work When:
   247	
   248	❌ Distinct feature with a separate outcome
   249	❌ Different requirement or separate user story outside the current scope
   250	❌ Alternative strategy that must remain independently governed
   251	❌ Complete redesign that supersedes rather than extends the current packet
   252	❌ Unrelated work with no requirement, implementation, or dependency continuity
   253	
   254	New/unrelated work is allowed to use Option B, but the new packet must still be evaluated independently. If it meets both phase thresholds, create a new phased packet; otherwise create a standard packet.
   255	
   256	**Phased Packets:** If the target already has child phase folders, use the active child when the work fits that child's scope. For a distinct but related workstream, prefer Option C and add the next sequential child phase instead of creating a new top-level sibling packet. Use the coordination root only for phase-map updates, root-scope decisions, and tree-truth verification. Use Option B only when the work is outside the phase parent's documented purpose and scope.
   257	
   258	**Small-task definition:** "Small" means exempt work or work that remains Level 1 after LOC guidance and all secondary risk/complexity factors are applied. A Level 2 or Level 3 task that does not phase-qualify is not automatically "small"; it remains standard because the phase thresholds were not both met.
   259	
   260	---
   261	
   262	## 9. CONFIRMATION OPTIONS
   263	
   264	When Gate 3 applies, always present all four stable labels and ask the user to choose:
   265	
   266	**Option A:** Continue in the detected/current spec or current phase child when its scope fits.
   267	**Option B:** Create a new top-level packet for new/unrelated work; independently evaluate whether that new packet qualifies for phases.
   268	**Option C:** Use another existing packet — a related spec when it is a better scope match, a specific child under an existing phase parent, a related standard packet decomposed into phases when it meets both phase thresholds, or a series parent when the work is a different change to the same artifact as an existing packet in the same track (phase-definitions.md §2).
   269	**Option D:** Skip spec documentation after the required warning or under an existing exemption.
   270	
   271	**Recommendation priority:** `C` for a distinct related workstream in an existing or qualifying phased packet, including a series parent; otherwise `A` when the current packet fits; otherwise `C` for another related packet; `B` only for new/unrelated work; `D` is never the default.
   272	
   273	**AI Agent Rule:** Recommend the best-fit option using this priority, but NEVER select it autonomously. The user must choose A/B/C/D.
   274	
   275	---
   276	
   277	## 10. LEVEL MIGRATION (PROGRESSIVE ENHANCEMENT)
   278	
   279	### Script-Assisted Upgrade (Recommended)
   280	
   281	```bash
   282	# Upgrade to Level 2 (auto-detects current level)
   283	bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/upgrade-level.sh specs/042-feature/ --to 2
   284	
   285	# Upgrade to Level 3 (chains through intermediate levels)
   286	bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/upgrade-level.sh specs/042-feature/ --to 3
   287	
   288	# Upgrade to Level 3+
   289	bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/upgrade-level.sh specs/042-feature/ --to 3+
   290	
   291	# Preview changes first
   292	bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/upgrade-level.sh specs/042-feature/ --to 3 --dry-run
   293	```
   294	
   295	**Post-Upgrade:** After the script runs, AI **must** auto-populate all `<placeholder_token>` text in newly injected sections by reading existing spec context and deriving appropriate content.
   296	
   297	Then verify placeholders are fully resolved:
   298	
   299	```bash
   300	.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/check-placeholders.sh specs/042-feature/
   301	```
   302	
   303	### Manual Fallback
   304	
   305	| From | To | Files to Add |
   306	|------|----|--------------|
   307	| 1 → 2 | Add verification | acceptance-criteria.md |
   308	| 2 → 3 | Add decision documentation | decision-record.md (+ optional research/research.md) |
   309	| 3 → 3+ | Add governance | Extended governance sections + AI protocols |
   310	
   311	**Always:**
   312	- Update `level:` field in metadata
   313	- Add changelog entry noting escalation
   314	- Keep existing documentation (progressive - don't delete)
   315	- Inform user of level change
   316	
   317	---
   318	
   319	## 11. TROUBLESHOOTING
   320	
   321	### "I forgot to create the spec folder"
   322	
   323	**Fix:**
   324	1. Stop coding immediately
   325	2. Create spec folder retroactively
   326	3. Document what was done and why
   327	4. Get user approval
   328	5. Continue with documentation in place
   329	
   330	---
   331	
   332	### "I'm not sure which level to choose"
   333	
   334	**Solution:**
   335	- When in doubt → choose **higher level**
   336	- Ask user if confidence <80%
   337	- Consider complexity and risk, not only LOC
   338	- Better to over-document than under-document
   339	
   340	---
   341	
   342	### "Can I change levels mid-work?"
   343	
   344	**Yes:**
   345	- Going up: Add additional files (see Level Migration table)
   346	- Going down: Keep existing docs (uncommon)
   347	- Always: Inform user why level changed, update changelog
   348	
   349	---
   350	
   351	### "What if it's only exploration?"
   352	
   353	**Rule:**
   354	- Pure exploration/reading = NO spec needed
   355	- Once you write/edit ANY files = SPEC REQUIRED
   356	- If uncertain → create spec (safer)
   357	
   358	---
   359	
   360	### "Do I need specs for documentation changes?"
   361	
   362	**YES - Documentation changes require specs the same as code changes.**
   363	
   364	**Requires spec:**
   365	- ✅ Code files (*.js, *.ts, *.css, *.py)
   366	- ✅ Documentation files (*.md, *.txt, docs/)
   367	- ✅ Configuration files (*.json, *.yaml, *.toml)
   368	- ✅ Knowledge base files
   369	- ✅ Templates (.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/templates/*.md)
   370	- ✅ Build files (package.json, requirements.txt)
   371	
   372	**Exceptions (no spec needed):**
   373	- ❌ Single typo fix (<5 characters in one file)
   374	- ❌ Whitespace-only changes
   375	- ❌ Auto-generated updates (package-lock.json)
   376	
   377	---
   378	
   379	### "When do I need an Architecture Decision Record (ADR)?"
   380	
   381	**Create `decision-record-*.md` when making:**
   382	- Database, framework, or library choices
   383	- Architectural pattern selections
   384	- Major refactoring approaches
   385	- Infrastructure/deployment strategy changes
   386	
   387	**Format:** Use descriptive name (e.g., `decision-record-database-choice.md`)
   388	
   389	---
   390	
   391	## 12. WHAT REQUIRES SPEC FOLDERS
   392	
   393	| File Type | Requires Spec | Examples |
   394	|-----------|--------------|----------|
   395	| Code files | ✅ Yes | JavaScript, TypeScript, Python, CSS, HTML |
   396	| Documentation | ✅ Yes | Markdown, README updates, guides |
   397	| Configuration | ✅ Yes | JSON, YAML, TOML, .env templates |
   398	| Knowledge base | ✅ Yes | Project-specific knowledge files |
   399	| Templates | ✅ Yes | `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/templates/*.md` modifications |
   400	| Build/tooling | ✅ Yes | package.json, requirements.txt, Dockerfile |

