    13	
    14	# Worked Examples Reference
    15	
    16	Practical scenarios demonstrating SpecKit workflows for different documentation levels.
    17	
    18	---
    19	
    20	## 1. OVERVIEW
    21	
    22	### What Are Worked Examples?
    23	
    24	These examples demonstrate real-world usage of the SpecKit system across different complexity levels and scenarios. Each example shows the complete flow from Gate 3 (spec folder question) through implementation and completion, providing practical templates you can follow.
    25	
    26	**Core Purpose**:
    27	- **Learn by Example** - See complete workflows in action
    28	- **Level Selection** - Understand when to use Level 1, 2, or 3
    29	- **Resume Patterns** - Learn how to continue interrupted work
    30	
    31	### Core Principle
    32	
    33	> "Every spec folder tells a story - from problem to solution with documented decisions."
    34	
    35	### Example Overview
    36	
    37	| Example | Scenario | Level | LOC | Key Learning |
    38	|---------|----------|-------|-----|--------------|
    39	| Simple Bug Fix | CSS alignment issue | 1 | ~50 | Minimal documentation |
    40	| Feature Implementation | Notification system | 2 | ~300 | Checklist-driven QA |
    41	| Architecture Change | REST to GraphQL migration | 3 | ~800 | Decision records |
    42	| Resuming Work | Continue previous session | N/A | N/A | Context recovery |
    43	
    44	---
    45	
    46	## 2. SIMPLE BUG FIX (LEVEL 1)
    47	
    48	### Scenario
    49	
    50	Fix a CSS alignment issue affecting button positioning on mobile viewports. Estimated scope: ~50 lines of code.
    51	
    52	**Why Level 1**: Small, focused change with clear acceptance criteria. No complex dependencies or architectural decisions.
    53	
    54	### Workflow
    55	
    56	**Phase 1: Initiate with Command**
    57	
    58	```bash
    59	User: /spec_kit:complete
    60	AI: Spec folder? A) Existing B) New (new or unrelated work only) C) Related (Use a related folder, phase child, or a series parent for a different change to the same artifact as an existing packet in the same track) D) Skip
    61	User: B
    62	```
    63	
    64	**Phase 2: Provide Details**
    65	
    66	```bash
    67	AI: Feature name?
    68	User: fix-button-alignment
    69	AI: Documentation level? (Recommended: 1 based on scope)
    70	User: 1
    71	AI: Creating specs/008-fix-button-alignment/ with Level 1 templates...
    72	```
    73	
    74	**Phase 3: Implementation**
    75	
    76	The AI creates the spec folder and begins implementation following the generated plan.
    77	
    78	### Files Created
    79	
    80	```markdown
    81	specs/008-fix-button-alignment/
    82	├── spec.md      # Problem description, acceptance criteria
    83	├── plan.md      # CSS changes needed
    84	├── tasks.md     # [ ] Update button.css, [ ] Test in browsers
    85	├── implementation-summary.md  # Final state, validation evidence
    86	└── scratch/     # (empty)
    87	```
    88	
    89	### Key Takeaways
    90	
    91	| Aspect | Learning |
    92	|--------|----------|
    93	| Level Selection | Level 1 is appropriate for <100 LOC with clear scope |
    94	| Templates | Minimal set: spec.md, plan.md, tasks.md |
    95	| No closure gate | Level 1 skips acceptance-criteria.md; verification lives in tasks.md |
    96	| Speed | Fast setup, immediate implementation |
    97	
    98	---
    99	
   100	## 3. FEATURE IMPLEMENTATION (LEVEL 2)
   101	
   102	### Scenario
   103	
   104	Add a user notification system with toast messages, persistence, and dismissal functionality. Estimated scope: ~300 lines of code.
   105	
   106	**Why Level 2**: Medium complexity feature requiring QA validation. Multiple components involved but no architectural decisions.
   107	
   108	### Workflow
   109	
   110	**Phase 1: Planning**
   111	
   112	```bash
   113	User: /spec_kit:plan
   114	AI: Spec folder? 
   115	User: B (New)
   116	AI: Feature name?
   117	User: notification-system
   118	AI: Documentation level? (Recommended: 2 based on ~300 LOC estimate)
   119	User: 2
   120	```
   121	
   122	**Phase 2: Implementation**
   123	
   124	```bash
   125	User: /spec_kit:implement specs/009-notification-system/
   126	AI: Loading planning artifacts...
   127	AI: Found: spec.md, plan.md, acceptance-criteria.md
   128	AI: Ready to implement. Starting with Phase 1 tasks...
   129	```
   130	
   131	### Files Created
   132	
   133	```markdown
   134	specs/009-notification-system/
   135	├── spec.md        # Feature requirements, acceptance criteria
   136	├── plan.md        # Implementation approach, component breakdown
   137	├── tasks.md       # Granular task list with priorities
   138	├── acceptance-criteria.md   # the closure gate
   139	├── implementation-summary.md  # Final state, validation evidence
   140	└── scratch/       # Debug logs, test scripts
   141	```
   142	
   143	### Key Takeaways
   144	
   145	| Aspect | Learning |
   146	|--------|----------|
   147	| Two-Phase | Separating plan and implement allows review |
   148	| Closure gate | Level 2 adds acceptance-criteria.md, which decides whether the packet may close |
   149	| Multi-Session | `/speckit:save` routes continuity into packet docs for later recovery |
   150	| Priority Tags | P0/P1/P2 in the tasks.md verification region guide completion order |
   151	
   152	---
   153	
   154	## 4. ARCHITECTURE CHANGE (LEVEL 3)
   155	
   156	### Scenario
   157	
   158	Migrate from REST API to GraphQL for improved data fetching efficiency. Estimated scope: ~800 lines of code with high risk.
   159	
   160	**Why Level 3**: Significant architectural change affecting multiple systems. Requires documented decision rationale for future reference.
   161	
   162	### Workflow
   163	
   164	**Phase 1: Initiate Planning**
   165	
   166	```bash
   167	User: /spec_kit:plan
   168	AI: Spec folder?
   169	User: B (New)
   170	AI: Feature name?
   171	User: graphql-migration
   172	AI: Documentation level? (Recommended: 3 based on ~800 LOC and architectural impact)
   173	User: 3
   174	```
   175	
   176	**Phase 2: Decision Record Creation**
   177	
   178	Level 3 requires an Architecture Decision Record (ADR):
   179	
   180	```markdown
   181	# ADR-001: GraphQL Migration
   182	
   183	## Status
   184	Approved
   185	
   186	## Context
   187	Current REST API has N+1 query problems causing performance issues.
   188	Mobile clients over-fetch data, increasing bandwidth usage.
   189	
   190	## Decision
   191	Migrate to GraphQL with Apollo Server for the API layer.
   192	
   193	## Consequences
   194	
   195	### Positive
   196	- Reduced over-fetching
   197	- Better developer experience
   198	- Single endpoint simplicity
   199	
   200	### Negative
   201	- Learning curve for team
   202	- Migration period complexity
   203	- Caching strategy changes
   204	```
   205	
   206	### Files Created
   207	
   208	```markdown
   209	specs/010-graphql-migration/
   210	├── spec.md            # Migration scope, success criteria
   211	├── plan.md            # Phased migration approach
   212	├── tasks.md           # Detailed task breakdown
   213	├── acceptance-criteria.md  # the closure gate
   214	├── decision-record.md # ADR documenting rationale
   215	├── implementation-summary.md # Final state, validation evidence
   216	└── scratch/           # Migration scripts, test queries
   217	```
   218	
   219	### Key Takeaways
   220	
   221	| Aspect | Learning |
   222	|--------|----------|
   223	| Decision Record | Level 3 requires decision-record.md (ADR format) |
   224	| Risk Documentation | Consequences section captures trade-offs |
   225	| Rollback Planning | Checklist includes rollback validation |
   226	| Future Reference | ADR serves as historical documentation |
   227	
   228	### Phased Coordination Root (Level 3+)
   229	
   230	Scenario: Large multi-phase effort requiring root coordination.
   231	1. Create root spec folder (Level 3+): `specs/022-feature/`
   232	2. Root `spec.md` is a coordination document with point-in-time snapshot: directory counts, phase status
   233	3. Create child phases: `specs/022-feature/001-epic/`, `specs/022-feature/002-indexing/`
   234	4. Each child has Parent Spec, Predecessor, Successor navigation metadata
   235	5. Implementation/resume happens in the active child - root is coordination-only
   236	6. Completion: validate recursively, verify root phase-map matches live children
   237	
   238	---
   239	
   240	## 5. RESUMING WORK
   241	
   242	### Scenario
   243	
   244	Continue work on a feature from a previous session where context was saved.
   245	
   246	**Why This Matters**: Long-running features span multiple sessions. Context recovery prevents rework and maintains momentum.
   247	
   248	### Workflow
   249	
   250	**Phase 1: Resume Command**
   251	
   252	```bash
   253	User: /speckit:resume specs/009-notification-system/
   254	```
   255	
   256	**Phase 2: Context Recovery**
   257	
   258	```bash
   259	AI: Loading context for specs/009-notification-system/
   260	    Found: handover.md (from 2 hours ago)
   261	    
   262	    CONTINUATION - Attempt 2
   263	    Last: Completed notification component
   264	    Next: Add notification API endpoint
   265	    Blockers: None
   266	    
   267	    Progress: 60% (6/10 checklist items)
   268	    
   269	    Ready to continue. Starting with: Add notification API endpoint
   270	```
   271	
   272	### Files Created
   273	
   274	| File | Purpose |
   275	|------|---------|
   276	| `handover.md` | Session state snapshot in the packet root |
   277	| `tasks.md` | Progress tracking (6/10 verification items) |
   278	| `tasks.md` | Next action identification |
   279	
   280	### Key Takeaways
   281	
   282	| Aspect | Learning |
   283	|--------|----------|
   284	| Handover Files | Maintained through `/speckit:save` and packet continuity refreshes before session end |
   285	| Progress Tracking | Checklist shows completion percentage |
   286	| Blocker Awareness | Resume surfaces any documented blockers |
   287	| Seamless Continuation | No need to re-explain context |
   288	
   289	---
   290	
   291	## 6. RELATED RESOURCES
   292	
   293	### Reference Files
   294	
   295	| File | Purpose |
   296	|------|---------|
   297	| level specifications reference | Detailed level requirements and thresholds |
   298	| [template-guide.md](../templates/template-guide.md) | Template usage and customization |
   299	| [folder-structure.md](../structure/folder-structure.md) | Spec folder organization |
   300	
   301	### Commands Reference
   302	
   303	| Command | Purpose |
   304	|---------|---------|
   305	| `/speckit:complete` | Full workflow (plan + implement) |
   306	| `/speckit:plan` | Planning phase only |
   307	| `/speckit:implement` | Implementation with existing plan |
   308	| `/speckit:resume` | Continue from previous session |
   309	| `/speckit:save` | Save context before ending session |
   310	
   311	### Level Selection Guide
   312	
   313	| LOC | Risk | Level | Key File Added |
   314	|-----|------|-------|----------------|
   315	| <100 | Low | 1 | (base set only) |
   316	| 100-499 | Medium | 2 | acceptance-criteria.md |
   317	| 500+ | High | 3 | decision-record.md |
   318	
   319	> **Note**: Risk and complexity can override LOC thresholds. When in doubt, choose the higher level.
   320	
   321	---

