    12	version: 1.6.0.7
    13	---
    14	
    15	# Auto Mode Contract Reference
    16	
    17	This reference module defines the single canonical `:auto` setup-resolution contract used by every user-invokable `/command:*:auto` surface in spec-kit (`/spec_kit:*`, `/create:*`, `/deep:*`). Each command's §0 cites this contract and supplies only its own per-field Default Resolution Table + `PRE-BOUND SETUP ANSWERS:` field list. The contract semantics, marker grammar, error format, and verification protocol live here.
    18	
    19	---
    20	
    21	## §1. Three-Tier Flow
    22	
    23	`:auto` mode does NOT mean "never ask" (too rigid) and does NOT mean "always ask the full setup block" (too soft). It means "ask only when uncertain or ambiguous." This is achieved via three tiers, evaluated in order:
    24	
    25	### Tier 1 — Resolve Confidently
    26	
    27	When `execution_mode = AUTONOMOUS` (from `:auto` suffix), the command's §0 MUST attempt to resolve every required input from these sources in order:
    28	
    29	1. **`$ARGUMENTS` flags** — explicit user-passed flags (e.g. `--max-iterations=10`, `--spec-folder=PATH`, command-specific flags)
    30	2. **`PRE-BOUND SETUP ANSWERS:` block in the prompt body** — see §2 for grammar. Caller's explicit prompt-body binding wins over `$ARGUMENTS` when both are present
    31	3. **Positional-scope spec-folder extraction** — for any `spec_folder`-class field: if the positional scope / `$ARGUMENTS` contains a path token that canonicalizes (alias roots resolved) to an EXISTING spec folder under `specs/` or `.opencode/specs/`, bind the field to that folder and STRIP the token from the scope. Match against the folder discovery the command already runs (e.g. `find specs .opencode/specs -mindepth 2 -maxdepth 2 -type d`). A spec folder named inline is a CONFIDENT resolution — NOT a Tier-2 ambiguity and NOT a Tier-3 absence. An explicit `--spec-folder` flag or marker (sources 1–2) still wins over an inline path.
    32	4. **Documented per-field defaults** — see the command's own Default Resolution Table (§3) for which fields carry sensible defaults
    33	
    34	After resolution: if every required field has a value, persist the resolved map to the command's working-config file (e.g. `deep-review-config.json`, `plan-config.json`), set `STATUS: PASSED`, load the paired YAML workflow. Do NOT emit any setup question. End §0.
    35	
    36	**Standalone / no-folder fallback guard (fail-closed):** when a command offers a standalone (no-spec-folder) output mode, that mode is valid ONLY when no spec folder was named in the scope or otherwise derivable. If source 3 (or an interactive choice) identified a spec folder, the standalone mode MUST NOT be selected — bind the identified folder and write the packet under `{spec_folder}/`. Preflight MUST fail closed if a standalone target is bound while a spec folder was identifiable from the scope.
    37	
    38	### Tier 2 — Targeted Ask
    39	
    40	Use Tier 2 ONLY when Tier 1 leaves one or two required inputs unresolved AND each unresolved field is genuinely ambiguous AND no sensible default exists.
    41	
    42	Constraints:
    43	- Emit ONE narrow question per ambiguous field, naming only the ambiguous field. NEVER the full consolidated Q-block from the legacy setup phase.
    44	- If two fields are ambiguous but one's resolution likely makes the other obvious (e.g. `review_target_type` may make `spec_folder` self-evident), ask the first one first; do not bundle multiple ambiguities into a single prompt.
    45	- Missing required input (e.g. empty `$ARGUMENTS` with no marker for a required positional) is NOT ambiguity. Absence is a Tier 3 case.
    46	- Wait briefly for user reply. If an answer arrives, merge it into the resolved map and re-run Tier 1 from the default-application step. If stdin is closed (`</dev/null`, redirected output, non-interactive dispatch) and no answer arrives, fall through to Tier 3. Never wait indefinitely.
    47	
    48	### Tier 3 — Fail Fast
    49	
    50	Use Tier 3 when `:auto` still has unresolved required inputs after Tier 1 and Tier 2, OR when Tier 2 produced no answer because stdin is closed.
    51	
    52	Emit the named-missing-inputs error format (§4), exit non-zero, do NOT load the YAML workflow, do NOT emit any further setup question.
    53	
    54	---
    55	
    56	## §2. PRE-BOUND SETUP ANSWERS Schema
    57	
    58	The `PRE-BOUND SETUP ANSWERS:` block is a structured marker the dispatched AI parses out of the prompt body. It allows callers to pre-bind setup field values without using individual `$ARGUMENTS` flags.
    59	
    60	Grammar (YAML-style key/value, one field per line):
    61	
    62	```
    63	PRE-BOUND SETUP ANSWERS:
    64	  <field_name>: <value>  # optional inline comment
    65	  <field_name>: <value>
    66	  ...
    67	```
    68	
    69	Rules:
    70	
    71	- The block opens with `PRE-BOUND SETUP ANSWERS:` (exact case-sensitive heading, followed by colon). Indentation MAY be inside a markdown fenced code block or inline in the prompt text.
    72	- Each field is `  <key>: <value>` indented two spaces, one per line.
    73	- Values are interpreted by the command's Default Resolution Table (§3). Field types and value enums are command-specific.
    74	- Comments after `#` on a value line are ignored.
    75	- Unknown fields produce a warning (do not abort), so callers may include forward-compatible fields.
    76	- Fields not specified fall back to: command-specific Default Resolution Table → command-specific default value → unresolved (triggers Tier 2 or Tier 3 per §1).
    77	- Malformed lines (e.g. missing `:`, malformed value type) emit a parse error naming the line. Known fields parsed before the error may still be used; remaining unresolved fields continue to Tier 2 or Tier 3.
    78	- A second `PRE-BOUND SETUP ANSWERS:` block in the same prompt body produces a duplicate-block error.
    79	
    80	The marker block applies ONLY under `:auto`. Under `:confirm`, the block is informational and does not skip the consolidated question prompt.
    81	
    82	---
    83	
    84	## §3. Default Resolution Table
    85	
    86	Every command MUST publish a Default Resolution Table in its §0 (after citing this contract). The table lists each setup field with the following columns:
    87	
    88	| Field | Required | Resolves Via | Default | Tier-2 Candidate |
    89	|-------|----------|--------------|---------|------------------|
    90	
    91	Column meanings:
    92	
    93	- **Field**: the field name (matches the schema in §2 and the question label in the legacy consolidated Q-block).
    94	- **Required**: `Y` (must be resolved before YAML load) or `N` (optional).
    95	- **Resolves Via**: ordered list of resolution sources, e.g. `flag → $ARGUMENTS` / `marker → PRE-BOUND` / `scope-extract → spec-folder path named in scope (§1 source 3)` / `default → "<value>"` / `auto-detect from <other field>` / `requires-ask`. Any `spec_folder`-class field MUST list `scope-extract` so a folder named inline binds at Tier 1.
    96	- **Default**: the documented default value when no flag, marker, or auto-detection applies. May be `none` (no default — Tier 2 or Tier 3 candidate).
    97	- **Tier-2 Candidate**: `Y` if a missing value can be resolved by ONE targeted question (per §1); `N` if absence triggers fail-fast (Tier 3).
    98	
    99	A field is a Tier-2 candidate only when ALL of:
   100	- No flag exists for it.
   101	- No marker is provided.
   102	- No sensible default exists.
   103	- The field is genuinely required (Required=`Y`).
   104	- A user choice is the only resolution path (ambiguity, not absence).
   105	
   106	---
   107	
   108	## §4. Tier 3 Fail-Fast Error Template
   109	
   110	When Tier 3 fires, the command emits this exact format and exits non-zero:
   111	
   112	```
   113	[ERROR] /<command>:auto — required inputs unresolved under autonomous mode:
   114	  - <field>: <why-unresolved>
   115	  - <field>: <why-unresolved>
   116	Resolution: provide via $ARGUMENTS flags, PRE-BOUND SETUP ANSWERS: block, or switch to :confirm mode for interactive setup.
   117	```
   118	
   119	Rules:
   120	
   121	- Replace `<command>` with the canonical command name (e.g. `spec_kit:deep-review`).
   122	- One bulleted line per unresolved required field.
   123	- `<why-unresolved>` is one short phrase: "missing — no flag, no marker, no default" or "ambiguous — Tier 2 received no answer (stdin closed)".
   124	- Final line cites the three resolution paths verbatim.
   125	- Do NOT load the YAML workflow.
   126	- Do NOT emit setup questions in Tier 3.
   127	- Exit code: non-zero (1 is the convention).
   128	
   129	---
   130	
   131	## §5. `:confirm` Invariance
   132	
   133	The `:confirm` mode (interactive, with full consolidated Q-block) is unaffected by this contract. Commands MUST preserve their existing `:confirm` behavior exactly. The contract applies ONLY when `execution_mode = AUTONOMOUS` (from `:auto` suffix or explicit `--mode=AUTONOMOUS` flag).
   134	
   135	Test invariant: a `:confirm` dispatch against any contract-adopting command MUST emit the original consolidated Q-block (Q0..Qn). Regression-check this per command after migration.
   136	
   137	---
   138	
   139	## §6. Verification Protocol
   140	
   141	Each contract-adopting command requires four verification traces (dry-run mental dispatch traces against the command's §0):
   142	
   143	### Trace A — Tier 1 pass
   144	Dispatch with all required inputs resolvable (flags + markers + defaults). Expected: STATUS PASSED, YAML loaded, no question emitted.
   145	
   146	### Trace B — Tier 2 targeted ask
   147	Dispatch with exactly one ambiguous required field. Expected: ONE narrow question naming only that field; NEVER the full consolidated block.
   148	
   149	### Trace C — Tier 3 fail fast
   150	Dispatch with one or more truly missing required inputs (and stdin closed in non-interactive runtimes). Expected: error format from §4, exit non-zero, no YAML load.
   151	
   152	### Trace D — `:confirm` regression
   153	Dispatch with `:confirm` suffix and any inputs. Expected: existing full consolidated Q-block emitted.
   154	
   155	Live verification (when feasible) extends the dry-run traces with actual non-interactive dispatches (opencode `exec </dev/null`, opencode `run --pure ... </dev/null`, claude `--print`) capturing transcripts as evidence. Live verification confirms the contract works under real runtime stdin-closure conditions.
   156	
   157	Evidence schema (one file per dispatch):
   158	- Command invocation verbatim
   159	- Full transcript
   160	- Verdict footer: PASS / PARTIAL / FAIL / SKIP
   161	- Optional: timing, model, runtime gotchas observed
   162	
   163	Stored under `<packet>/evidence/<command-slug>-<trace>.txt`.
   164	
   165	---
   166	
   167	## §7. Adoption Checklist
   168	
   169	For each command adopting this contract, the §0 of the command markdown:
   170	
   171	- [ ] Adds a one-line citation: `Setup contract: see references/workflows/auto-mode-contract.md`
   172	- [ ] Provides its own per-field Default Resolution Table (§3) listing every Q-block field with the four columns
   173	- [ ] Provides its own `PRE-BOUND SETUP ANSWERS:` field list (one row per setup field, matching the Default Resolution Table)
   174	- [ ] Documents any command-specific Tier-2 ordering rule (e.g. "ask review_target_type before spec_folder")
   175	- [ ] Updates frontmatter `argument-hint` to reference the new bypass capability
   176	- [ ] Preserves the existing `:confirm` consolidated Q-block path untouched
   177	- [ ] Authors §0 dry-run traces (A/B/C/D) and stores them under the adopting packet's `evidence/`
   178	
   179	Commands that have adopted this contract:
   180	
   181	- `/deep:review` (prototype) — see `.../103/001-deep-review-three-tier-setup/`
   182	- (to be appended as `.../103/002-...` rolls out)
   183	
   184	---
   185	
   186	## §8. Out of Scope
   187	
   188	- Commands without paired YAML workflows (`/prompt`, `agent_router`, etc.) — they have no setup-phase surface where the contract applies.
   189	- Skill-internal `:auto` flows (sk-doc, sk-code routing, etc.) — those are skill SKILL.md surfaces, not `/command:*` surfaces.
   190	- YAML workflow internal interaction gates (e.g. mid-loop approval gates) — handled by each YAML's own non-interactivity rules, not this contract.
   191	- Cross-command `:auto` integration (e.g. `/spec_kit:plan:auto` chained into `/spec_kit:implement:auto`) — separate contract concern.
   192	
   193	## §9. PROVENANCE
   194	
   195	Origin: shipped from the deep-review prototype and generalized in the 12-command rollout.
   196	
   197	Related memory: feedback_auto_mode_ask_only_when_ambiguous.md, feedback_opencode_spawnagent_allowlist.md, feedback_gate3_no_tmp_exemption.md.

