     1	---
     2	title: Agent I/O Contract
     3	description: Optional agent dispatch headers and result envelopes for agent exchanges; advisory metadata never blocks a valid exchange.
     4	trigger_phrases:
     5	  - "agent io contract"
     6	  - "dispatch result envelopes"
     7	  - "agent handoff group"
     8	  - "advisory metadata fields"
     9	importance_tier: important
    10	contextType: implementation
    11	version: 1.6.0.8
    12	---
    13	
    14	# Agent I/O Contract
    15	
    16	Shared advisory contract for structured agent dispatch and return metadata. This contract helps orchestrators and leaf agents exchange typed hints without making older prompts invalid.
    17	
    18	## 1. CONTRACT STATUS
    19	
    20	```yaml
    21	schema_version: agent-io/v1
    22	status: optional-advisory
    23	```
    24	
    25	- Every field is optional unless a caller and receiver explicitly agree otherwise in the task prompt.
    26	- Absence of any dispatch header or result envelope is never a refusal condition.
    27	- Existing agent-specific output formats remain authoritative.
    28	- Consumers parse best-effort and ignore unknown fields.
    29	
    30	### Advisory Status For Design Handoffs
    31	
    32	Agent I/O is advisory-only: a convenience header that may opportunistically carry manifest or result digests as data, never as authority. The enforceable design gate is the design proof token plus the guarded boundary: guarded-proxy classification, the structured transport result, and parent re-validation. Presence of Agent I/O headers never substitutes for that gate, and absence of Agent I/O headers never passes a design handoff. Acceptance for this note is the advisory-only status, the real-gate naming, both failure directions, and the by-name pointer to `cli-child-pairing.md` "Agent I/O Is Not The Gate" as the canonical scoped statement; this prose adds no checker, schema, or refusal reason.
    33	
    34	---
    35	
    36	## 2. DISPATCH GROUP
    37	
    38	Orchestrators may place this compact header near the top of a delegated task prompt. Target size: under 15 lines.
    39	
    40	```text
    41	AGENT_IO_DISPATCH v1
    42	schema_version: agent-io/v1
    43	dispatch_id: <stable id or none>
    44	agent: @orchestrate | @context | @code | @review | @debug | @markdown | @prompt-improver | @ai-council | @deep-research | @deep-review
    45	task_type: explore | implement | review | debug | document | plan | research
    46	task_definition: <one-line task summary>
    47	context_snapshot: none | included | one-shot
    48	read_directives: none | packet-first | codebase-first | exact-paths
    49	contract: advisory
    50	expected_result: native | agent_io_result
    51	```
    52	
    53	Receivers treat these fields as routing hints. If a field conflicts with the loaded agent definition, the agent definition and runtime safety rules win.
    54	
    55	Optional extended dispatch fields may also ride in this header when an orchestrator has useful advisory context:
    56	
    57	```text
    58	context_package: none | included
    59	handoff: none | debug_to_implement
    60	pre_execution: none | scoped
    61	reviewer_focus: none | <comma-separated high-risk files, modules, or concerns>
    62	self_assessed_quality: none | high | medium | low | <short producer confidence note>
    63	```
    64	
    65	Consumers must keep these fields advisory and ignore unknown values rather than rejecting an otherwise valid task prompt.
    66	
    67	---
    68	
    69	## 3. RESULT GROUP
    70	
    71	Leaf agents may append this envelope after their required native output:
    72	
    73	```text
    74	AGENT_IO_RESULT v1
    75	schema_version: agent-io/v1
    76	dispatch_id: <matching dispatch_id or none>
    77	status: pass | fail | blocked | partial
    78	confidence_band: high | medium | low
    79	confidence_numeric: 0.90 | 0.70 | 0.30
    80	failure_type: none | unknown_stack | scope_conflict | low_confidence | logic_sync | verify_fail | p0 | p1 | p2 | missing_info | access_denied | complexity_exceeded | external_dependency | operator_opt_in_missing
    81	summary: <one-line outcome>
    82	files_changed: <comma-separated repo-relative paths or none>
    83	verification: <command/result, evidence summary, or not_applicable>
    84	next_action: <specific follow-up or none>
    85	```
    86	
    87	Rules for result envelopes:
    88	
    89	- Append after the native response body, never before a required first-line status.
    90	- Do not replace required sections such as a Context Package, review report, debug report, or code RETURN block.
    91	- `confidence_band` is the canonical human signal.
    92	- `confidence_numeric` is deterministic and derived from `confidence_band`: `high -> 0.90`, `medium -> 0.70`, `low -> 0.30`.
    93	- If a provided numeric value conflicts with the band mapping, consumers should trust the band and recompute the numeric value.
    94	- Use `files_changed: none` for read-only agents.
    95	- Use `verification: not_applicable` only when the agent's native contract allows no verification command.
    96	- Keep summaries factual and evidence-backed.
    97	
    98	---
    99	
   100	## 4. HANDOFF GROUP
   101	
   102	Optional handoff fields describe information crossing from one agent to another. The first concrete use is a debug-to-implementation handoff. This is a narrower, advisory adaptation of Gem's existing orchestrator `debugger_diagnosis` machine-check; it adds typed fields without replacing native debug reports.
   103	
   104	```text
   105	AGENT_IO_HANDOFF v1
   106	schema_version: agent-io/v1
   107	handoff_type: debug_to_implement | none
   108	source_agent: @debug | none
   109	target_agent: @code | none
   110	root_cause: <evidence-backed cause, or none>
   111	target_files: <comma-separated repo-relative paths, or none>
   112	fix_recommendations: <short recommended fix steps, or none>
   113	confidence: high | medium | low | none
   114	```
   115	
   116	Handoff rules:
   117	
   118	- This group is required only when an orchestrator explicitly hands an `@debug` diagnosis to `@code` for a diagnosis-based surgical fix.
   119	- `target_files` are recommendations, not an automatic edit allowlist. The orchestrator and receiver still enforce their scoped file rules.
   120	- If the crossing is present but required fields are missing, `@code` should return `BLOCKED` with low confidence rather than inventing a fix.
   121	- Legacy `debug-delegation.md` reports outside a debug-to-implementation crossing warn and require manual verification; they are not rejected for missing this group.
   122	- Absence of this group in ordinary work remains valid and must not block the receiver.
   123	
   124	---
   125	
   126	## 5. PRE-EXECUTION GROUP
   127	
   128	Optional pre-execution fields describe scoped gates that fire only when their predicate is true. The orchestrator owns the predicates and should not spread heuristic copies across leaf agents.
   129	
   130	```text
   131	AGENT_IO_PRE_EXECUTION v1
   132	schema_version: agent-io/v1
   133	diagnosis_crosses_agents: true | false
   134	change_class: docs | typo | ordinary | api | schema | integration | other
   135	boundary_contract: <contract, boundary test, executable acceptance check, or none>
   136	complexity: low | medium | high
   137	pre_mortem: none | risk=<low|medium|high>; failure_modes=<2-3 concise modes>; assumptions=<concise assumptions>
   138	```
   139	
   140	Pre-execution rules:
   141	
   142	- Gate A fires only when `diagnosis_crosses_agents: true`.
   143	- Gate B fires only when `change_class` is `api`, `schema`, or `integration`; it asks for a contract, boundary test, or executable acceptance check before production edits.
   144	- Gate C fires only when `complexity` is `medium` or `high`; low-complexity tasks omit `pre_mortem`.
   145	- A low, typo, or docs task with no cross-agent diagnosis should skip all three gates.
   146	- Missing pre-execution metadata in legacy or ordinary dispatches remains a degraded advisory state, not a rejection reason.
   147	
   148	---
   149	
   150	## 6. ADVISORY GROUP
   151	
   152	Optional advisory fields route review attention and surface spec drift without creating gates, mutations, or new refusal reasons.
   153	
   154	```text
   155	AGENT_IO_ADVISORY v1
   156	schema_version: agent-io/v1
   157	reviewer_focus: none | <comma-separated high-risk files, modules, or concerns>
   158	self_assessed_quality: none | high | medium | low | <short producer confidence note>
   159	spec_drift: none | reason=<one-line recommendation>; affected_spec_docs=<comma-separated docs or none>; update_recommended=<true|false>
   160	```
   161	
   162	Advisory rules:
   163	
   164	- `reviewer_focus` helps reviewers prioritize reads and evidence gathering. It never changes severity thresholds and never creates a finding without normal evidence.
   165	- `self_assessed_quality` is the producer's own confidence note for reviewer triage. It is not a review score and must not be named `quality_score`.
   166	- `spec_drift` is a recommendation to update packet docs later. It never edits spec docs automatically and never bypasses Logic-Sync for hard contradictions.
   167	- If `spec_drift` is absent, consumers should treat it as `none`.
   168	- Missing advisory fields in legacy or ordinary dispatches remain valid and must not block the receiver.
   169	
   170	---
   171	
   172	## 7. EVIDENCE GROUP
   173	
   174	Optional evidence fields let a load-bearing claim carry its proof in a fixed, machine-checkable shape at the dispatch boundary. This is the structural backstop for the doctrine that a finding is a hypothesis until verified: a claim either names its class and what would confirm it, or the missing piece is surfaced as an advisory. The schema is owned by `runtime/` (`lib/deep-loop/evidence-contract.ts`); that module is the source of truth for allowed values, and this doc cites it rather than redefining them.
   175	
   176	```text
   177	AGENT_IO_EVIDENCE v1
   178	schema_version: agent-io/v1
   179	claim_class: confirmed | inferred | hypothesis | unknown
   180	would_confirm: <what would confirm an inferred or hypothesis claim, or none>
   181	gate_delta: <baseline -> after gate result, e.g. "351 -> 357 passing">
   182	scope_state: in_scope | out_of_scope | scope_expanded
   183	child_result_verified: true | false
   184	```
   185	
   186	> **Representation (where this actually lives).** Unlike §2-§6, this block is NOT a free-standing `AGENT_IO_EVIDENCE` text envelope that agents append to their output — no agent currently emits one, and nothing parses one. The Evidence Group is carried as the structured `evidence` field on a deep-loop JSONL iteration record, and the only consumer validates it as a structured object via `validateEvidenceContract(record.evidence)` (`.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/lib/deep-loop/post-dispatch-validate.ts`). The fenced shape above is the documented field schema (keys + allowed values), not a literal text format to emit. Producing it as a text block appended to agent output would not be validated.
   187	
   188	Evidence rules:
   189	
   190	- The group is optional. A record that omits the evidence metadata entirely is valid and passes post-dispatch validation with no warning (absence never blocks).
   191	- When the metadata is present, all five fields are expected. A partial payload (some but not all five), a wrong-typed field, or an unknown enum value is reported as an advisory naming the offending `evidence.<field>` path; it never fails the exchange by default.
   192	- Validation is advisory by default (`DEEP_LOOP_EVIDENCE_ENFORCEMENT=warn`). `off` suppresses the advisories; `strict` marks them distinctly but still does not block, because no blocking failure reason is defined for evidence yet. Any future promotion to blocking is a separate, baseline-gated decision.
   193	- Evidence field values are treated as inert data. Consumers validate the shape only and never interpret a field's contents as instructions.
   194	
   195	## Compatibility
   196	
   197	- Senders may omit the dispatch header.
   198	- Receivers may omit the result envelope when their native format is sufficient.
   199	- Consumers must not reject a result solely because this advisory metadata is absent.

