     1	---
     2	title: SpecKit Manifest Extension Maintainer Guide
     3	description: Maintainer guide for adding new manifest-backed document types to the SpecKit template system.
     4	trigger_phrases:
     5	  - manifest extension
     6	  - spec-kit-docs.json
     7	  - document type addition
     8	  - template version workflow
     9	importance_tier: normal
    10	contextType: reference
    11	version: 1.0.0.0
    12	---
    13	
    14	# SpecKit Template Extension Guide
    15	
    16	> Every document type's `trigger_phrases` are judged at index time by the retrieval convention's negative classes. Author them per `../references/retrieval/retrieval-conventions.md` section 8: two or more tokens each, never a bare folder token.
    17	<!-- Lives at templates/ (not references/) because it co-locates with the manifest assets it documents. -->
    18	
    19	Private maintainer guide for adding a new manifest-backed document type.
    20	
    21	Use this guide when extending the manifest taxonomy, template version map, or level contract anchors while keeping docs and manifest entries aligned.
    22	
    23	---
    24	
    25	## 1. ADD A DOCUMENT TYPE
    26	
    27	1. Add the template file under `templates/` using the `*.md.tmpl` suffix.
    28	2. Add an entry in `spec-kit-docs.json.documents` with:
    29	   - `template`: the template filename.
    30	   - `owner`: `author`, `command`, `agent`, or `workflow`.
    31	   This section is a descriptive index for readers. Nothing reads it at
    32	   runtime: the scaffolder and the validator both work from the `levels`
    33	   rows below, and a template is found by its `.tmpl` name in the role
    34	   folders. Keep the index truthful, but know that changing it changes no
    35	   behavior.
    36	3. Add the template filename to `spec-kit-docs.json.versions`.
    37	4. Add the document name to the appropriate level row:
    38	   - `requiredCoreDocs` for files every packet at that level must have before implementation starts.
    39	   - `requiredAddonDocs` for level add-ons that must always be present.
    40	   - `optionalAddonDocs` for documents a level recognises without the
    41	     file-presence rule hard-erroring on them, such as
    42	     `acceptance-criteria.md`. Presence for these is owned by whichever rule
    43	     understands the rollout, because the file-presence rule has no notion of
    44	     when a packet was created.
    45	   - `lazyAddonDocs` for files a packet gets only on request. The list is
    46	     identical across the four numbered levels; phase parents drop the two
    47	     documents a workflow writes into a child, and review and research
    48	     packets drop `goal.md` as well. It mixes two ownership models: documents an
    49	     author asks for (`--with-lazy-addons` scaffolds four of them,
    50	     `--with-goal` one, the inline gate renderer any of them) and documents a
    51	     command or workflow writes (`handover.md`, `debug-delegation.md`,
    52	     `research/research.md`).
    53	   - `lifecycleRequiredDocs.<transition>` for files required after a documented lifecycle transition.
    54	5. Add per-document anchors under `levels.<level>.sectionGates.<doc-name>`.
    55	6. Run the Level contract and golden snapshot tests.
    56	
    57	---
    58	
    59	## 2. VERSION WORKFLOW
    60	
    61	Template versions live in `spec-kit-docs.json.versions[<template basename>]`.
    62	Writers should emit `SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE` markers with the current manifest version.
    63	Readers must accept legacy marker formats indefinitely, including v2.1 inline markers.
    64	
    65	Version bump policy:
    66	
    67	- Patch-level content edits that do not change anchors may keep the same marker version.
    68	- Header, anchor, required document, or frontmatter contract changes require a version bump.
    69	- New document types start at the current manifest version.
    70	
    71	---
    72	
    73	## 3. MANIFEST SCHEMA REFERENCE
    74	
    75	`documents` describes the private document taxonomy.
    76	`levels` describes which documents are active for each level.
    77	`requiredCoreDocs` and `requiredAddonDocs` describe unconditional documents; `lifecycleRequiredDocs` describes documents that become required at a named lifecycle transition such as `afterImplementationStarts`.
    78	`levels.<level>.sectionGates` is a per-document anchor profile. Keep legacy flat keys only when compatibility requires it; new anchors belong under the document basename.
    79	`versions` is the source of truth for template version checks.
    80	
    81	---
    82	
    83	## 4. VALIDATION CHECKLIST
    84	
    85	- `resolveLevelContract(level).templateVersions` exposes the new template version.
    86	- `serializeLevelContract()` preserves `sectionGatesByDocument`.
    87	- `scaffold-golden-snapshots.vitest.ts` snapshots the rendered output.
    88	- `validate.sh --strict` passes on fresh scaffolds for every level touched.
     1	---
     2	title: SpecKit Manifest Migration Maintainer Guide
     3	description: Maintainer guide for legacy template marker support and manifest migration compatibility policy.
     4	trigger_phrases:
     5	  - manifest migration
     6	  - legacy marker support
     7	  - spec-kit-docs.json
     8	  - template compatibility policy
     9	importance_tier: normal
    10	contextType: reference
    11	version: 1.0.0.0
    12	---
    13	
    14	# SpecKit Template Migration Guide
    15	<!-- Lives beside spec-kit-docs.json under templates/ (not references/) because it co-locates with the manifest it documents. -->
    16	
    17	Private maintainer notes for template marker and frontmatter compatibility.
    18	
    19	Use this guide when maintaining manifest compatibility with legacy packets, marker formats, and current-version write policy.
    20	
    21	---
    22	
    23	## 1. LEGACY MARKER SUPPORT
    24	
    25	Legacy markers such as `<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: spec-core | v2.1 -->` remain supported indefinitely.
    26	Readers should parse the template basename and version when present, but they must not reject historical packets only because the marker predates v2.2.
    27	
    28	Writers always emit the current manifest-backed marker version from `spec-kit-docs.json.versions`.
    29	
    30	---
    31	
    32	## 2. BROAD DOCUMENT LIST DERIVATION
    33	
    34	When a legacy packet does not have manifest-aware metadata, derive its document list from files on disk:
    35	
    36	- Always include existing core docs: `spec.md`, `plan.md`, `tasks.md`, and `implementation-summary.md`.
    37	- Include add-on docs when present: `decision-record.md` and `resource-map.md`.
    38	- Include command-owned docs only when present: `handover.md`, `debug-delegation.md`, and `research/research.md`.
    39	- For phase parents, prefer the lean trio: `spec.md`, `description.json`, and `graph-metadata.json`.
    40	
    41	The derived list is read-only compatibility data. Do not rewrite old packets only to normalize marker style.
    42	
    43	---
    44	
    45	## 3. SUNSET POLICY
    46	
    47	There is no planned sunset date for v2.1 marker parsing.
    48	The migration policy is indefinite read support plus current-version writes.
    49	
    50	---
    51	
    52	## 4. NEVER INVENT PROVENANCE
    53	
    54	A tool writes a template-source marker only when the document's anchors exactly equal the anchor set the template renders for the packet's level. A document that does not match keeps no marker, and no marker or comment is ever added to record unknown provenance.
    55	
    56	`check-template-staleness.sh --auto-upgrade` and `quality-audit.sh --fix` were removed, and `upgrade-legacy.mjs` is the repair path.
    57	
    58	---
    59	
    60	## 5. EXTENSION PROCESS LINK
    61	
    62	For new document types, follow `EXTENSION-GUIDE.md` and add both the manifest document entry and a per-document `sectionGates` profile.
     1	---
     2	title: "Packet Changelog Templates"
     3	description: "Packet-local changelog templates for root specs and phase child folders."
     4	trigger_phrases:
     5	  - "packet changelog templates"
     6	  - "nested changelog templates"
     7	  - "root phase changelog"
     8	---
     9	
    10	# Packet Changelog Templates
    11	
    12	---
    13	
    14	## 1. OVERVIEW
    15	
    16	`templates/changelog/` contains packet-local changelog templates generated inside spec folders. These templates summarize canonical packet docs and are separate from global release-note templates under `.skilled/changelog/`.
    17	
    18	Current state:
    19	
    20	- `root.md` renders changelogs for root spec folders.
    21	- `phase.md` renders changelogs for phase child folders.
    22	- Recovery flows through `/speckit:resume`, implemented by `.skilled/commands/speckit/resume.md`, and canonical continuity docs.
    23	
    24	---
    25	
    26	## 2. DIRECTORY TREE
    27	
    28	```text
    29	changelog/
    30	├── README.md
    31	├── root.md    # Root packet changelog template
    32	└── phase.md   # Phase child changelog template
    33	```
    34	
    35	---
    36	
    37	## 3. KEY FILES
    38	
    39	| File | Role |
    40	|---|---|
    41	| `root.md` | Template for `changelog/changelog-<packet>-root.md`. |
    42	| `phase.md` | Template for phase child changelogs in the parent packet `changelog/` folder. |
    43	
    44	---
    45	
    46	## 4. BOUNDARIES AND FLOW
    47	
    48	Boundaries:
    49	
    50	- This directory owns packet-local changelog templates only.
    51	- It must not replace global release-note templates.
    52	- Generated changelogs summarize packet docs, not live worktree diffs.
    53	
    54	Generation flow:
    55	
    56	```text
    57	╭────────────────────╮
    58	│ canonical spec docs│
    59	╰─────────┬──────────╯
    60	          ▼
    61	┌────────────────────┐
    62	│ root.md or phase.md│
    63	└─────────┬──────────┘
    64	          ▼
    65	┌────────────────────┐
    66	│ changelog generator│
    67	└─────────┬──────────┘
    68	          ▼
    69	┌────────────────────┐
    70	│ packet changelog   │
    71	└────────────────────┘
    72	```
    73	
    74	---
    75	
    76	## 5. ENTRYPOINTS
    77	
    78	| Entrypoint | Use |
    79	|---|---|
    80	| `root.md` | Root spec folder changelog generation. |
    81	| `phase.md` | Phase child changelog generation. |
    82	| Changelog generator script | Reads canonical packet docs and applies one template. |
    83	
    84	---
    85	
    86	## 6. VALIDATION
    87	
    88	Run from the repository root after template edits:
    89	
    90	```bash
    91	bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/validate.sh <spec-folder> --strict
    92	```
    93	
    94	Generated changelogs should reference available packet docs and avoid claiming task completion beyond those docs.
    95	
    96	---
    97	
    98	## 7. RELATED
    99	
   100	| Document | Role |
   101	|---|---|
   102	| [sk-doc changelog template](../../../sk-doc/sk-create-changelog/assets/changelog-template.md) | Global changelog and release-note template. |

